    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32982": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of the Practice",
      "description": "Duke University, African and African American Studies Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, African and African American Studies Position ID: Duke-AAAS-POP [#32982] Position Title: Assistant Professor of the Practice Position Type: Non tenure-track faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27701, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Black Studies Appl Deadline: 2026/12/01 23:59:59  (posted 2026/10/08 , updated 2026/10/06 ) Position Description: Apply Position Description Job Advertisement for Assistant Professor of the Practice, Career Track Field: History and Production of Hip-Hop \r\nStart Date: July 1, 2027 \r\nEnd Date: June 30, 2031 The Department of African & African American Studies (AAAS) at Duke University in Durham, NC invites applications for a career-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor of the Practice specializing in the history and production of hip-hop music. Applicants should be prepared to teach large and small undergraduate classes on topics including the history of hip-hop, hip-hop production, and Black popular culture. A bachelor’s degree in a related field is required for consideration. Applicants must also have a graduate degree(s) and/or specialized creative expertise in the music industry. The candidate should have a strong record of successful teaching at the undergraduate level, evidence of scholarly and/or creative production, and familiarity with the various developments and debates within Black Studies. In addition to teaching two classes per semester, or four per year, the candidate must continue to develop their scholarly and/or creative portfolio, contributing further to the academic ecosystem of the department. The candidate will also be expected to provide service to the department and university. This includes attending department meetings and events, mentoring and advising students, serving on committees, and being part of a robust intellectual and social community. Applicants should submit the following required materials via ( https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo ): cover letter including teaching statement and description of scholarly and/or creative work, curriculum vitae, three sample syllabi, two course evaluations if available, and three letters of recommendation. \r\n \r\nAll applications received by December 1, 2026, will receive full consideration. Inquiries about the position should be addressed to the chair of the search committee, Charmaine Royal at charmaine.royal@duke.edu. Duke University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment opportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. Duke aspires to create a community built on collaboration, innovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on the robust exchange of ideas – an exchange that is best when the rich diversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching statement Three Three Sample  Syllabi Description of Scholarly and/or Creative Work Two Course Evaluations Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) Further Info: www.aaas.duke.edu 1316 Campus Drive 243 Friedl Building Box 90252 Durham, NC  27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32111": {
      "title": "Associate In Research",
      "description": "Duke University, Biology Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Biology Position ID: Duke-Biology-AIR_DS [#32111] Position Title: Associate In Research Position Type: Other Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Biology Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/05/26 , listed until 2026/11/26) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Sherwood Lab, part of the Department of Biology at Duke University, is seeking a full-time Associate in Research to join a research program focused on understanding how basement membranes regulate tissue development, maintenance, aging, and disease. The lab studies basement membranes, specialized extracellular matrices that surround and support tissues and organs. Using the model organism Caenorhabditis elegans, we combine genetics, CRISPR-based genome engineering, live imaging, and quantitative microscopy to investigate how basement membranes are assembled, remodeled, and maintained in vivo. To support these efforts, the postgraduate researcher will learn and perform a variety of experimental procedures, including CRISPR-based genome editing, molecular cloning, C. elegans genetics, fluorescence microscopy, live-cell imaging, quantitative image analysis, and molecular biology techniques. The researcher will work closely with the PI and other lab members to support ongoing projects and develop independent research skills. Applicants should be motivated, dependable, highly organized, and able to work effectively within a collaborative research environment. Strong communication skills and careful attention to detail are essential. This position is an excellent opportunity for individuals seeking additional research experience before pursuing graduate, medical, or professional school training. Minimum requirements: Bachelor’s degree in biology, cell biology, genetics, biochemistry, bioengineering, or a related field. Prior research experience in molecular biology, genetics, microscopy, image analysis, or model organisms is highly desirable. The position is available beginning Summer 2026, with flexible start dates based on candidate availability. For more information, visit the Sherwood Lab website. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.biology.duke.edu 137 Biological Sciences Bldg. Box 90338 Durham NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/31974": {
      "title": "Associate in Research",
      "description": "Duke University, Biology Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Biology Position ID: Duke-Biology-AIR_MO [#31974] Position Title: Associate in Research Position Type: Other Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Biology Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/04/21 , listed until 2026/10/21) Position Description: Apply Position Description A salaried, full-time Associate in Research position with excellent benefits is available in the Onishi lab in the Department of Biology at Duke University. The Onishi lab uses the green alga Chlamydomonas reinhardttii to study cell division and its coordination with chloroplast division. The Associate in Research will contribute to a project focused on identifying novel components involved in chloroplast division through proteomics and genetics. Skills related to genetic analysis, molecular biology, imaging, and computational analysis are required, and experience with microalgae and/or yeasts is ideal. Responsibilities will include (but not be limited to) media and reagent preparation, molecular biology, algal and bacterial culture, genetic crosses and genotyping, phenotypic analysis, microscopy, protein sample preparation, and data analysis. This position is part of a research team, and training and mentoring will be provided. The AIR will also mentor 1-2 undergraduates conducting independent research. This is an excellent opportunity for highly motivated recent college graduates interested in a career in research. This position requires a BS/BA degree. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Curriculum Vitae Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.biology.duke.edu 137 Biological Sciences Bldg. Box 90338 Durham NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
