    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32134": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Duke University, Biology Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Biology Position ID: Duke-Biology-PD_SJ [#32134] Position Title: Postdoctoral Researcher Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Biology Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/06/01 , listed until 2026/12/01) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Johnsen lab at Duke University is seeking a postdoctoral researcher to investigate the ultrastructural and structural correlates of muscle transparency in several species of freshwater fish. Applicants should hold a PhD in Biology or a related field and have experience working with various forms of imagery such as confocal, microCT, and possibly lightsheet and SEM/TEM. We are particularly interested in self-motivated individuals who can interact well with a multi-national and multi-disciplinary team. The position can start as early as July 1, 2026. Interested applicants should submit the following documents to sjohnsen@duke.edu: 1. A cover letter describing how the candidate’s interests align with the research focus of the Johnsen lab 2. A current CV 3. A list of references Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.biology.duke.edu 137 Biological Sciences Bldg. Box 90338 Durham NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32112": {
      "title": "PostDoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Duke University, Biology Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Biology Position ID: Duke-Biology-PD_XD [#32112] Position Title: PostDoctoral Associate Position Type: Other Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Biology Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/05/26 , updated 2026/05/27 , listed until 2026/11/26) Position Description: Apply Position Description A salaried full-time postdoc position is available in the Dong laboratory at Duke University. The candidate will contribute to the central, but understudied, mechanism of plant adaptation, which is redox signaling of reactive oxygen species (ROS), particularly hydrogen peroxide (H2O2). Beyond serving as a metabolic byproduct of stress, H2O2 is a mobile signal that coordinates stress responses, such as systemic acquired resistance (SAR), an immune mechanism that primes uninfected tissues against future infection by a broad spectrum of pathogens. While the downstream effects of SAR, including salicylic acid (SA)-mediated gene expression, are well studied, the molecular relay that enables H2O2 to initiate systemic SA biosynthesis remains poorly understood. A central unresolved question is: how H2O2-dependent signaling is perceived and amplified to trigger systemic SA production under climate-relevant stress. This project seeks to uncover the molecular mechanisms by which H2O2 mediates systemic immune signaling in plants under environmental stress, a process critical for plant resilience and food security in a changing climate. The project will involve performing molecular biology and plant-microbial interaction experiments, with the specific experiments in studying the effect of redox/ROS signaling. The candidate will also be expected to be involved in training and supervision of students and outside visitors involved with this project. Full-time research experience and molecular biology skills are necessary, and computational skills are highly desirable. The application should include your cover letter and CV with your last name. A minimum of a Ph.D. degree is required for the position. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.biology.duke.edu 137 Biological Sciences Bldg. Box 90338 Durham NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/31971": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Duke University, Biology Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Biology Position ID: Duke-Biology-RA_AS [#31971] Position Title: Research Associate Position Type: Other Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Biology Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/04/21 , listed until 2026/10/21) Position Description: Apply Position Description An NSF-funded research associate position is available in the Schmid lab, Duke University Biology Department. A PhD is required in fields including but not limited to bioengineering, genomics, biostatistics, microbiology, computer science, bioinformatics, and/or related fields. Candidates with strong computational skills and a background in microbial physiology, network modeling, bioinformatics, genome sequencing, OMICS analysis, systems biology, optimization theory, statistics, and computer programming are preferred. Clear evidence of past research productivity is required, and strong scientific and interpersonal communication skills are expected. Our lab values the ability to work together with colleagues and teach and mentor students from diverse backgrounds and perspectives. In the position, the successful candidate will use machine learning to model and predict how microbes use transcription to dynamically adapt to stress and other perturbations. The successful candidate will analyze the results of the model and be expected to write research papers communicating the results. To apply, please upload the following documents to this link in Academic Jobs Online (LINK): · A letter of interest that describes your qualifications for the job, anticipated contributions, and your experience contributing to inclusive environments. · Your curriculum vitae. · Contact information for three professional references. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.biology.duke.edu 137 Biological Sciences Bldg. Box 90338 Durham NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32994": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Duke University, Biology Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Biology Position ID: Duke-Biology-TTAP_SH [#32994] Position Title: Tenure Track Assistant Professor Position Type: Tenured/Tenure-track faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Biology Appl Deadline: ( accepting applications posted 2026/10/13 , updated 2026/10/07 , listed until 2026/11/30) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Department of Biology at Duke University in Durham, NC, invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Cellular and Molecular Systems. The initial appointment begins July 1, 2027, and ends June 30, 2031, and is renewable upon successful review in the third year. We seek scholars who investigate the principles governing living systems, including minimal rules underlying synthetic systems, the rules of interactions among molecules, cells, and organisms across environmental conditions, and rules of emergence and complexity in biological networks and circuits. The candidates will combine molecular, biochemical, cellular, biochemical and/or genetic approaches with quantitative and/or computational tools in the study of these systems. Successful candidates will establish an independent, nationally recognized, externally funded research program and contribute to a vibrant and collaborative scientific community at Duke University. The candidates will also integrate research, teaching, mentoring, and service into a unified academic program, engaging students and researchers at all levels in the process of scientific discovery and drawing on their perspectives and creativity to enrich their research. Duke Biology is the intellectual home for more than 45 faculty members whose research programs span the discipline of Biology (including developmental biology, cell biology, molecular biology, ecology, evolution, organismal biology, and genomics). Many of our biologists take advantage of our department’s Research Greenhouses and Phytotron, Duke University core facilities (e.g., microscopy, genomics, proteomics), or conduct research within the 7000-acre Duke Forest Teaching and Research Laboratory or the Duke Marine Lab. More than 100 PhD students work with Biology faculty through the Biology PhD program and multiple interdisciplinary training programs. Our undergraduate major graduates ~175 students annually, with more than half of our majors conducting independent research. Learn more about Duke Biology at our website https://biology.duke.edu/ . How to Apply: Applicants should hold a Ph.D. degree in biology or one of its subfields. Interested candidates should submit the following required documents: - a cover letter (with contact information); - curriculum vitae - A research statement that includes a description of research accomplishments and plans for future research (3 pages maximum) - Teaching and mentoring statement that includes a description of teaching and mentoring\r\nphilosophy, previous experiences, and future plans (1-2 pages) - Service and outreach statement that includes a description of approaches, training, and experiences in leading and participating in recruitment programs, research groups, classrooms, mentoring, and service interactions (1 page). - Three letters of recommendation Applications must be submitted through Academic Jobs Online: Application review will begin on November 02, 2026, but we will continue to accept applications through November 30, 2026. For additional questions or concerns, please contact Dr. Paul Magwene, Search Committee Chair, biochairs@duke.edu Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Teaching statement Service/Outreach Statement Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.biology.duke.edu 137 Biological Sciences Bldg. Box 90338 Durham NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32682": {
      "title": "BME/CAGT Junior Tenure-Track Faculty",
      "description": "Duke University, Biomedical Engineering Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Biomedical Engineering Position ID: Duke-BME-BMECAGTJUNIORFACULTY [#32682] Position Title: BME/CAGT Junior Tenure-Track Faculty Position Type: Tenured/Tenure-track faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Biomedical Engineering / Biomedical Engineering Appl Deadline: 2026/11/02 23:59:59  (posted 2026/09/14 , listed until 2027/03/14) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Duke University Department of Biomedical Engineering in the Pratt School of Engineering and the Center for Advanced Genomic Technologies are seeking applicants for an open tenure-track faculty position. Applications are welcome from investigators at all career stages but early career researchers including those pursuing their first independent position are particularly encouraged to apply. We are seeking collaborative and cross-disciplinary scientists and engineers doing innovative work in all areas of molecular and cellular technology development and its application to the diagnosis, treatment, and/or fundamental understanding of human disease. Particular research areas of interest include the development and application of machine learning and artificial intelligence methods to better model, interpret, predict, and design biomolecular and cellular structure and function. We are especially interested in work that bridges computational and experimental approaches as well as research areas with translational potential. Additional areas of interest include, but are not limited to: genome engineering, functional genomics/epigenomics including high-throughput genetic screening and/or epigenomic profiling and annotation, methods to study genome structure, genome and nucleic acid imaging, cell reprogramming, precision medicine, and gene and cell therapies including new delivery methods. Applicants are expected to complement other collaborating researchers with interests in basic and applied research in the areas of regenerative biology and medicine, cancer biology, pharmacology, immunology, neurobiology, and cell biology. The Duke Center for Advanced Genomic Technologies ( https://cagt.pratt.duke.edu ), an initiative established by the Pratt School of Engineering and School of Medicine in 2019, is a robust and dynamic center that builds upon Duke’s strengths in these areas of research as well as collaborations throughout the Schools of Engineering, Arts and Sciences, and Medicine. The CAGT is housed in the vibrant research community of the Fitzpatrick Center for Interdisciplinary Engineering, Medicine, and Applied Sciences and supports research and educational programs of scientists and clinicians across campus. The CAGT will partner with the Department of Biomedical Engineering (BME) and other Duke departments appropriate to the research program of selected applicants for this position. For over 50 years, Duke Biomedical Engineering has led the field in innovation. Today, this top-ranked program spans cutting-edge research in biomaterials, bioelectric engineering, biomedical imaging, computational medicine, cellular engineering, and synthetic/systems biology. Duke BME is dedicated to improving human health by developing technologies that support precision medicine, preventive care, and health equity while addressing chronic disease and the responsible use of health data. Duke BME consistently ranks among the nation's top programs thanks to our collaborative community of pioneering faculty, dedicated staff, and inspired students. Information on the multidisciplinary research and teaching in Biomedical Engineering can be found at https://bme.duke.edu/ . Duke University, the Department of Biomedical Engineering, and the Center for Advanced Genomic Technologies are strongly committed to advancing inclusive excellence throughout our research, teaching, and service activities. Duke aspires to create a community built on collaboration, innovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich diversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility to uphold these values.Interested candidates should submit the following by November 2, 2026:\r\n• cover letter (with contact information);\r\n• curriculum vitae;\r\n• research statement (no more than 3 pages) that includes a description of past research accomplishments, plans for future research, and description of past experiences demonstrating your commitment to community building and outreach;\r\n• teaching and mentoring statement (no more than 3 pages) describing teaching philosophy, previous experiences, future plans for teaching, and description of past experiences promoting and supporting a community building and outreach; and\r\n• list of three references who will provide letters on behalf of the candidate; note,letters will not be requested until after the first round of application evaluations. Applicants should have an earned doctorate in Biomedical Engineering, Computational Biology, Genetics/Genomics, or a related field. Applicants must apply through Academic Jobs Online https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32682 . Review of applications will begin immediately; applications received by Monday, November 2, 2026 will receive priority attention. All applications received past this date will be considered until the position is filled. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Teaching statement Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://www.bme.duke.edu https://www.bme.duke.edu © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32784": {
      "title": "BME NeuralEngineerng Tenure-Track Faculty",
      "description": "Duke University, Biomedical Engineering Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Biomedical Engineering Position ID: Duke-BME-BMENEAURLENGINEERING [#32784] Position Title: BME NeuralEngineerng Tenure-Track Faculty Position Type: Tenured/Tenure-track faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Biomedical Engineering / Biomedical Engineering Appl Deadline: 2026/12/01 23:59:59  (posted 2026/09/21 , listed until 2027/03/21) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Duke University Department of Biomedical Engineering (Duke BME) in the Pratt School of Engineering is seeking applicants for an open tenure-track faculty position in neural engineering . Applications are welcome from investigators at all career stages , but early career researchers including those pursuing their first independent position are particularly encouraged to apply . We encourage applications in the broadly defined areas of neuroscience coupled to technological and bio medical in no vation, computational modeling and analysis of the brain at all scales, non-invasive imaging or stimulation of the nervous system, and novel tools and applications for clinical translation. We are open to o ther areas of neural engineering as well. Duke BME is a driving force in creating engineering knowledge and novel biomedical technology that improve the human condition through the advancement of clinical care and biomedical science. The D epartment is within walking distance of Duke University School of Medicine and Duke University Hospital, and we work closely with medical professionals to identify important clinical problems that impact human health and solve them using our technical expertise. Information on the cutting edge, multidisciplinary research and teaching in Duke BME can be found at https://bme.duke.edu/ . Duke University and Duke BME are strongly committed to advancing inclusive excellence throughout our research, teaching, and service activities. For more on Duke BME’s culture and aspirations, see our Strategic Vision: https://bme.duke.edu/about/vision/ . Interested candidates should submit the following by December 1, 2026 : • cover letter (with contact information); • curriculum vitae; • research statement (no more than 3 pages) that includes a description of past research accomplishments, plans for future research, and description of past experiences demonstrating your commitment to community building and outreach; • teaching and mentoring statement (no more than 3 pages) describing teaching philosophy, previous experiences, future plans for teaching, and description of past experiences promoting and supporting a community building and outreach; and • list of three to five references who will provide letters on behalf of the candidate; note, letters will not be requested until after the first round of application evaluations. Applicants should have an earned doctorate in biomedical engineering, bioengineering, neuroscience, electrical engineering, chemical engineering, mechanical engineering, physics, or a related field . Applicants must apply through Academic Jobs Online : https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32784 \"> https:// academicjobsonline.org /ajo/jobs/32784 . Review of applications will begin immediately; applications received by December 1, 2026 will receive priority attention. All applications received past this date will be considered until the positions are filled. Do you have questions about the search? We can help – please send questions via email to the search committee chair , Dr. Marc Sommer, at marc.sommer@duke.edu . Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Teaching statement Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://www.bme.duke.edu https://www.bme.duke.edu © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
