    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32510": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Duke University, Civil and Environmental Engineering Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Civil and Environmental Engineering Position ID: Duke-Civil and Environmental Engineering-PDA_CMHUB1 [#32510, 50284570] Position Title: Postdoctoral Associate Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Civil and Environmental Engineering / Civil and Environmental Engineering Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/08/28 , updated 2026/08/25 , listed until 2027/03/01) Position Description: Apply Position Description Postdoctoral Associate, Critical Minerals Hub at Duke The Critical Minerals Hub at Duke invites applications for a full-time\r\npostdoctoral associate to conduct cutting-edge research in engineering and\r\nscience related to critical minerals. This position will contribute to building\r\ninterdisciplinary excellence and research pathways in critical minerals\r\nresearch at Duke University. We are seeking applications from researchers with\r\na demonstrated track record in developing independent research and who are\r\nmotivated to work at the nexus of multiple disciplines. The postdoctoral associate will develop a\r\nresearch project in collaboration with faculty related to critical minerals.\r\nPotential project topics are diverse, drawing on the expertise of Hub faculty in engineering (e.g.,\r\nenvironmental engineering, mechanical engineering and materials science)\r\n and\r\nscience (e.g., geoscience, geochemistry, environmental chemistry). A \r\nprimary advisor and co-advisor will represent distinct disciplines. The\r\npostdoctoral associate will be expected to pursue research projects \r\nwithin the\r\nHub faculty lab(s) and lead the presentation of their findings in \r\npeer-reviewed\r\npublications and in diverse forums (e.g., conferences, seminars). In addition to the primary research project,\r\nthe individual will be expected to engage in collaborative research, mentoring\r\n(graduate and undergraduate students), and education activities related to the\r\nCritical Minerals Hub. The postdoctoral associate will gain valuable insights\r\ninto center-level research and education activities, support in seeking funding for\r\nresearch, and the opportunity to build grantsmanship skills, engage with\r\ndiverse external partners in academia and industry, and gain mentorship from\r\nmultiple faculties across disciplines. Qualifications The successful applicant will possess a PhD or\r\nequivalent doctoral degree in an engineering or physical science discipline\r\nwith some research experience related to critical minerals. They\r\nmust be able to communicate the applicability of their prior research\r\nexperiences to the critical minerals thematic area of the Hub and describe the\r\ninterdisciplinary importance of these research experiences. Applicants\r\nshould have an excellent academic record and thrive in a collaborative\r\nmultidisciplinary research environment. The\r\ncandidate should be able to, and be excited about, working in a varied lab\r\ngroup setting; plan and conduct assessments independently and collaboratively;\r\ninterpret data; and present results to diverse audiences. Fluency\r\nin English (written and oral) is required. Excellent\r\nscientific writing skills and the ability to organize data and clearly\r\ndisseminate findings and scientific concepts are essential for success in this\r\nposition. The candidate must be able to demonstrate these skills. The initial appointment will be for one year\r\nwith the possibility of extension. Salary will be commensurate with\r\nexperience. Application review will begin on October 1, 2026, and \r\nwill be conducted on a rolling basis until the position is filled. The anticipated start date is January 1, 2027, but may be moved out depending on Hub and candidate discussions. Required Materials Curriculum vitae Cover letter, up to two pages. Introduce\r\n yourself and explain why this postdoctoral opportunity interests you. \r\nIn addition to describing your relevant experience, address the \r\nfollowing: What draws you to critical minerals and materials as a research area? What perspective, expertise, or way of thinking would you bring to the CM Hub at Duke? What\r\n is an example of a time you worked across disciplinary boundaries, \r\nentered an unfamiliar area, or helped connect people or ideas that are \r\nnot usually brought together? Finally, add something about your interests, motivations, or working style that we would not learn from your CV. Career development statement - one page. Describe the\r\ncareer you hope to build, the capabilities or experiences you would like to\r\ndevelop during the postdoctoral appointment, and how this position could help\r\nyou move toward those goals. We encourage applicants to identify what a\r\nsuccessful postdoctoral experience would look like for them. Research vision - one page. Identify one or\r\n     two research questions or opportunities related to critical minerals and\r\n     materials that you find especially compelling. We are particularly\r\n     interested in ideas that connect disciplines, methods, or parts of the\r\n     critical-minerals system. Briefly explain why the question matters, how\r\n     you might begin approaching it, and what expertise or collaborations would\r\n     strengthen the work. These ideas are intended to help us understand your\r\n     creativity and research instincts; they will not be treated as a fixed proposal\r\n     or commitment. Name and contact information for at least three professional references. References will be contacted only at a later stage of the search. Applicants\r\nare welcome to use figures, conceptual diagrams, or other concise visual\r\nelements in their statements when these help communicate their ideas. For questions about this position,\r\nplease email criticalminerals@duke.edu , and someone from our leadership team\r\nwill respond. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Career Development Statement Research Vision Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) Further Info: https://criticalminerals.duke.edu/ 121 Hudson Hall Box 90287 Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32798": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate in Photocatalysis and Green Chemistry",
      "description": "Duke University, Civil and Environmental Engineering Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Civil and Environmental Engineering Position ID: Duke-Civil and Environmental Engineering-PDA_GC [#32798, 51187817] Position Title: Postdoctoral Associate in Photocatalysis and Green Chemistry Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Civil and Environmental Engineering / Environmental Engineering Environmental Science & Engineering Appl Deadline: 2026/11/01 23:59:59 * (posted 2026/09/23 ) Position Description: Apply Position Description POSTDOCTORAL ASSOCIATE IN PHOTOCATALYSIS AND GREEN CHEMISTRY Department of Civil & Environmental Engineering The Gilbertson Laboratory invites applications for a full-time postdoctoral associate to advance a green chemistry approach to recovering critical metals from technology waste. We seek a researcher motivated to connect fundamentals of chemistry to developing an approach that reduces reliance on concentrated acids, bases, and other hazardous reagents. Research Focus and Responsibilities The project examines how photocatalyst chemistry, feedstock composition, and solvent properties jointly control metal liberation from solid matrices and subsequent metal fate. The postdoctoral associate will help develop and lead research in the following connected areas: Synthesize and characterize carbon nitride photocatalysts to link composition, bonding, and electronic structure with the generation and fate of photogenerated electrons, holes, and reactive oxygen species. Design controlled kinetic experiments to identify the redox pathways that enable metal liberation from chemically distinct solids and determine how catalyst and feedstock chemistry affect reaction rates and selectivity. Investigate solvent effects on interfacial reactions, reactive species behavior, and metal speciation. Distinguish release from the original feedstock from subsequent complexation, precipitation, colloid formation, adsorption, or redeposition. Integrate thermodynamic and chemical speciation predictions with kinetic measurements, solid-phase characterization, and metal mass balances to identify favorable reaction conditions and guide experimental and reactor design. The postdoctoral associate will be expected to lead peer-reviewed publications, present at conferences and workshops, collaborate with other researchers, and mentor graduate and/or undergraduate students. The appointment will provide opportunities to develop independent research questions within the project, build grantsmanship skills, and pursue professional development aligned with the individual’s career goals. Qualifications Applicants must hold a PhD or equivalent doctoral degree in chemistry, chemical or environmental engineering, materials science, or a closely related field by the start of the appointment. A strong experimental background in photochemistry, heterogeneous catalysis, materials chemistry, or a related area is required, along with the ability to formulate mechanistic hypotheses, design controlled experiments, and interpret quantitative data. Excellent scientific writing, clear oral communication in English, and the ability to work independently and collaboratively are essential. Additional Relevant Experience Experience in one or more of the following areas is particularly relevant. Applicants are not expected to have expertise in every area: Photocatalyst synthesis and characterization of composition, surface chemistry, electronic structure, or charge-carrier behavior, using methods such as XPS, UV–vis NIR, photoluminescence, or microscopy. Reactive species measurements, chemical probe assays, or mechanistic studies of redox and interfacial reactions. Metal analysis by ICP-MS or ICP-OES, solid-phase characterization, and mass balances across dissolved, colloidal, and solid fractions. Solution chemistry, solvent effects, thermodynamic or speciation modeling, or development of photocatalytic reactors. Appointment The initial appointment will be for one year with the possibility of extension. Salary will be commensurate with experience. The anticipated start date is January 1, 2027, and is negotiable. Application review will begin on October 1, 2026, and will continue on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Application Curriculum vitae Cover letter of up to two pages. Please introduce yourself and explain why this postdoctoral opportunity interests you. Describe the experience you would bring to the project, including your contributions to experimental design, methods development, or mechanistic interpretation. Discuss what draws you to the connections among photocatalysis, green chemistry, and critical metal recovery; an example of how you approached an unfamiliar research problem or worked across disciplines; and something about your motivations or working style that we would not learn from your CV. One-page career development statement. Describe the career you hope to build, the capabilities or experiences you would like to develop during the appointment, and how this position could help you move toward those goals. Applicants are encouraged to identify what a successful postdoctoral experience would look like for them. One-page research vision. Identify one or two questions within the project’s scope that you find compelling. These might address catalyst structure and reactivity, solvent effects on metal liberation, or the relationship between predicted chemical behavior and experimental observations. Briefly explain why the question matters, how you would begin investigating it, and what additional expertise or methods would strengthen the work. These ideas will help us understand your creativity and research instincts; they will not be treated as a fixed proposal or commitment. Names and contact information for at least three professional references. We will contact references only at a later stage of the search. Applicants are welcome to use figures, conceptual diagrams, or other concise visual elements in their statements when these help communicate their ideas. Gilbertson Laboratory at Duke University: https://gilbertsongroup.pratt.duke.edu/ . Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Career Development Statement Research Vision Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) Further Info: https://www.cee.duke.edu/ 121 Hudson Hall Box 90287 Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32806": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Duke University, Computer Science Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Computer Science Position ID: Duke-CS-POSTDOC2 [#32806] Position Title: Postdoctoral Associate Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Computer Science / Natural Language  Processing , Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning, Large Language Models Appl Deadline: 2026/12/31 23:59:59  (posted 2026/09/23 , listed until 2027/03/23) Position Description: Apply Position Description The DukeNLP group in the Department of Computer Science at Duke University invites applications for a postdoctoral associate position in natural language processing and machine learning. The group studies how language models acquire, use, and communicate information, and how these systems can be evaluated and deployed responsibly in consequential settings. We are particularly interested in understanding how errors arise and compound in extended or agentic workflows, and how humans can efficiently assess and verify AI-generated work. Possible research directions include developing methods and evaluations for factual and robust language generation, studying the behavior of models and agents over long-horizon tasks, and measuring or reducing the human effort required to verify AI-assisted outputs. Related work on the provenance, transparency, and accountability of human-AI-produced artifacts is also welcome. The specific research agenda will be developed jointly with the successful candidate. The postdoctoral associate will be encouraged to take intellectual ownership of one or more projects, develop new research directions, publish at leading NLP and machine learning venues, and release useful datasets, models, or evaluation resources. The researcher will also collaborate with graduate and undergraduate students and may participate in interdisciplinary projects with researchers across Duke and other institutions. Applicants should hold, or expect to complete before the start date, a PhD in computer science, machine learning, natural language processing, or a closely related field. Candidates should have: •\tA demonstrated record of original research in NLP, machine learning, or a related area •\tExperience conducting empirical research with large language models •\tStrong programming and experimental skills •\tThe ability to formulate and pursue research questions independently •\tStrong written and oral communication skills Experience with human-centered evaluation, reinforcement learning or post-training, agentic systems, or large-scale data collection is helpful but not required. We welcome candidates whose background complements the group’s existing expertise and who are interested in shaping a broader research program around reliable and accountable AI.\r\nThe postdoctoral associate will be supervised by Professor Bhuwan Dhingra and will be part of the broader NLP and AI research community at Duke. More information is available at https://nlplab.cs.duke.edu/ . The initial appointment is for one year, with the possibility of renewal for a second year contingent on satisfactory performance, continued funding, and mutual interest. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Further Info: https://www.cs.duke.edu (919) 660-6500 Department of Computer Science Box 90129 Duke University Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32185": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associates, Bartesaghi Lab - Duke University",
      "description": "Duke University, Computer Science Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Computer Science Position ID: Duke-CS-RA [#32185] Position Title: Postdoctoral Research Associates, Bartesaghi Lab - Duke University Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Structural Biology Computer Science / Machine Learning , Computer Vision , High Performance Computing , Machine learning methodology and applications Cryo-electron Microscopy Appl Deadline: 2026/09/30 23:59:59 * * (posted 2026/06/10 , listed until 2026/12/09) Position Description: Apply *** the listing date or deadline for this position has passed and new applications are no longer accepted. *** Position Description The Bartesaghi Lab ( https://cryoem.cs.duke.edu ) invites applications for two postdoctoral associate positions to develop computational methods and data analysis pipelines at the intersection of machine learning and cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM), cryo-electron tomography (cryo-ET), and sub-tomogram averaging, to address fundamental questions in the life sciences. Successful candidates will work at the interface of machine learning and experimental biology. Responsibilities include developing methods that embed domain knowledge into machine-learning models and applying these approaches to structural biology and disease-related questions, with particular emphasis on Parkinson’s disease. A central goal is scalable analysis of large datasets from dynamic complexes acquired with modern, high-throughput cryo-EM and in-situ cellular tomography workflows. Postdocs will join a collaborative, multidisciplinary team spanning Duke University (Computer Science; Electrical and Computer Engineering; Biochemistry; Neurobiology; and Cell Biology) and partner institutions, including the Van Andel Institute, Emory University, and the National Institute of Environmental Health Sciences (NIEHS). The Bartesaghi Lab has dedicated access to state-of-the-art research infrastructure, including cryo-EM and cryo-ET capabilities through the Pratt School of Engineering Shared Materials and Instrumentation Facility (SMIF), as well as high-performance CPU and GPU computing through Duke Research Computing. Ideal candidates should hold a PhD in Computer Science, Electrical and Computer Engineering, Biochemistry, Cell Biology, or a closely related area. Candidates must have at least one first-author or substantial-contribution publication in a leading venue in applied machine learning and/or structural biology and have strong recommendation letters from established researchers in the field. The application deadline is September 31, 2026, but applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis as received. Positions are for 2-4 years depending on the candidate’s interests and experience. Start dates are flexible, aligned with PhD completion. Duke University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment opportunities without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. Duke aspires to create a community built on collaboration, innovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on the robust exchange of ideas-an exchange that is best when the rich diversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Curriculum Vitae\r\nStatement of Research Experience/Interests\r\nTwo or more reference letters Further Info: Alberto Bartesaghi Department of Computer Science and Biochemistry\r\nDuke University\r\nDurham, NC 27708-0129\r\nBartesaghi Lab: https://cryoem.cs.duke.edu/ Working at Duke: https://hr.duke.edu/careers/about-duke Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Brief Statement of Research Experience/Interests Two or more reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://cryoem.cs.duke.edu 919-660-6579 Department of Computer Science Box 90129 Duke University Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32713": {
      "title": "Elliott and Griffiths Assistant Research Professors",
      "description": "Duke University, Department of Mathematics Skip to main content ‌ View Jobs Registered Employers Contact Us Help Login Duke University, Department of Mathematics Position ID: Duke-ARP2026 [#27193, 1537] Position Title: Elliott and Griffiths Assistant Research Professors Position Type: Non tenure-track faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Mathematics Appl Deadline: 2026/12/01 23:59:59 finished (2026/09/15, finished 2026/10/12) Position Description: *** this position has been closed and new applications are no longer accepted. *** Position Description Applications are invited for several positions as Elliott Assistant Research Professor of Mathematics and Griffiths Assistant Research Professor of Mathematics. Candidates should have completed a doctorate as of August 1, 2027 and show definite promise in research and teaching.  The teaching load for Elliott ARPs will be two courses during one semester and one course during the other, so that the appointee will have additional time for research.  The teaching load for Griffiths ARPs will vary, but will generally be one to three courses per academic year.  Elliott ARP appointments are for three years while Griffiths ARP appointments may be up to three years.  Appointments normally begin on August 1, 2027. Applicants are asked to submit (a) a vitae; (b) a research statement; and (c) a statement describing teaching interests and philosophy of mentoring.  Candidates with teaching portfolios are encouraged to submit them as well.  The applicant should request at least four letters of recommendation, including one that evaluates the applicant's teaching.  These letters should be uploaded, by their authors, at mathjobs.org or sent to the Duke University Department of Mathematics by January 1, 2027.  Each applicant is also requested to provide the name(s) of one or more members of the faculty of the Department of Mathematics at Duke who are working in the applicant's general area of research.  Applicants are encouraged to submit all of their materials at this site.  Applicants may also mail their materials to the Department. Applications received by December 1, 2026 will be guaranteed full consideration; early application is advisable. Duke University in Durham, North Carolina is an Affirmative Action / Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment opportunities without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status.  The department strives to create a welcoming, supportive environment for all of its members. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Teaching statement Four or more reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) Further Info: www.math.duke.edu 919-660-2800 Department of Mathematics Duke University Box 90320 Durham, NC 27708-0320 © 2026 MathJobs.Org, American Mathematical Society. All Rights Reserved."
