    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32973": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Care Studies and Scholarly Publishing",
      "description": "Duke University, History Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, History Position ID: Duke-History-PSTDOC [#32973] Position Title: Postdoctoral Fellow in Care Studies and Scholarly Publishing Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Feminist, Gender, and Sexuality Studies Global Studies Humanities and Social Sciences Appl Deadline: 2026/12/01 23:59:59 * (posted 2026/10/06 , listed until 2026/12/01) Position Description: Apply Position Description About the Position The ideal candidate for this position is a recent PhD with expertise in an aspect of the field of care studies and an interest in the development of open-access scholarly publishing and scholarly communication. This fellowship is particularly appropriate as a bridge from doctoral study into publishing. No prior professional editorial experience is required. Start date for this position will be June 1, 2027.  This is an in-person position. The Fellow will allocate 30 percent of their time to supporting RCGE’s scholarly digital projects, including the ongoing development of its digital community and the design and execution of a series of workshops intended to help authors prepare their articles for publication and 20 percent of their time to their own research and writing projects, which should have some connection to the broad field of care studies. The Fellow will allocate the other half of their time as the managing editor for Care in Common, a peer-reviewed, open-access journal launching under a pilot program to test the Libraries' capacity to provide editorial and hosting support for open-access journals. We expect to support the Fellow’s professional development and to train the successful candidate in editorial management systems, production standards, and the norms of journal operations. Responsibilities Digital Scholarly Projects Working-Papers workshops\r\n●\tWork with RCGE team to recruit participants in topical workshops.\r\n●\tMonitor submission and circulation of papers.\r\n●\tServe as the primary point of contact for all workshop participants. Digital Community Development\r\n●\tRecruit posts and assist in the production of the blog Care Talk.\r\n●\tContinue the development of the Directory of Researchers. \r\n●\tContribute to the production of the regular RCGE newsletter.\r\n●\tDevelop a library of pedagogical resources for those wanting to incorporate care studies into K-12 or post-secondary teaching.\r\nAssessment\r\n●\tAssessing website analytics to determine the impact of communications and dissemination strategies.\r\n●\tDevelop quantitative and qualitative tools to assess the impact of RCGE-supported scholarship during the NEH grant period.\r\n●\tWork with Directors and Grant Manager on final report of grant activities. Editorial Management Peer review management\r\n●\tConduct initial screening of submissions for scope, completeness, and adherence to journal guidelines; flag potential ethics or integrity concerns for the editors.\r\n●\tIdentify, invite, and secure qualified peer reviewers; monitor review timelines; send reminders; and keep authors informed of manuscript status.\r\n●\tManage the full editorial workflow in Scholastica, the journal's editorial management platform, including configuring forms, templates, and automated communications.\r\n●\tServe as the primary point of contact for authors, reviewers, and the editorial board on all process questions. Manuscript preparation and production handoff\r\n●\tCopyedit accepted manuscripts for grammar, clarity, consistency, and house style; verify citations and references.\r\n●\tPrepare final article files, including typeset PDFs and valid JATS XML, for delivery to Duke University Press and the Scholarly Publishing Collective.\r\n●\tCreate and deposit article metadata and ensure metadata quality supports discovery and indexing. Program development (pilot assessment)\r\n●\tDocument all workflows, timelines, and costs to create a replicable playbook for supporting additional journals.\r\n●\tTrack and report metrics (submission volume, time-to-decision, time-to-publication, reviewer acceptance rates) to inform the Libraries' assessment of the pilot.\r\n●\tRecommend process improvements, tools, and staffing models for a potential permanent editorial services program. Research & Writing \r\n●\tWork individually or in collaboration with the Revaluing Care team to pursue your own research and writing goals.\r\n●\tPresent a working paper (i.e., an article or chapter in progress) to the RCGE Working Papers Seminar.\r\n●\tContribute at least one blog post based on this paper. Required Qualifications\r\n●\tPhD in any discipline.\r\n●\tDemonstrated project management ability and exceptional attention to detail.\r\n●\tExcellent written communication skills and experience with scholarly writing conventions and citation practices.\r\n●\tExperience navigating academic peer review as an author, reviewer, or editorial assistant.\r\n●\tAbility to communicate diplomatically and professionally with scholars at all career stages.\r\n●\tWillingness to learn new technical skills, including editorial management software, XML markup, and metadata standards. Preferred Qualifications\r\n●\tSubject expertise related to the journal’s focus (care work, care ethics, feminist economics, labor studies, or adjacent fields).\r\n●\tExperience with journal editing, editorial assistance, or scholarly communication work in any capacity (including graduate journals).\r\n●\tFamiliarity with any of: Scholastica or comparable editorial management systems (OJS, ScholarOne, Editorial Manager); JATS XML or other structured markup (HTML, LaTeX, TEI); copyediting and style manuals (Chicago in particular); open-access publishing models and licensing (Creative Commons).\r\n●\tAbility to communicate in formal written Spanish and/or Portuguese. What the Fellowship Offers\r\n●\tMentorship and the opportunity to develop intellectual and professional networks in the field of care studies.\r\n●\tStructured training in editorial management, production standards, and publishing operations, with mentorship from Duke University Libraries staff and partners at Duke University Press.\r\n●\tDirect experience designing a publishing program from the ground up — experience directly relevant to careers in university presses, library publishing, scholarly communication, and editorial management.\r\n●\tThe opportunity to combine publishing work with continued scholarly engagement through the Revaluing Care in the Global Economy project. To Apply\r\nSubmit a CV including contact information for three references as well as a cover letter describing your interest in scholarly publishing, your connection to the field of care studies, and your own research objectives during the tenure of the postdoc.  Applications from candidates in all disciplines are welcome; we especially encourage applications from those whose scholarly interests connect to the journal's mission.  Materials are due via Academic Jobs Online by 1 December 2026. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site. Letters should be submitted by 2026/12/01 11:59PM ) Further Info: www.history.duke.edu 919-684-3014 224 Classroom Building (East Campus) Box 90719 Durham, NC 27708-0719 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32339": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Duke University, International Comparative Studies Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, International Comparative Studies Position ID: Duke-International Comparative Studies-RA [#32339, 51040592] Position Title: Research Associate Position Type: Other Position Location: durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: International Comparative Studies Appl Deadline: 2026/08/01 23:59:59  (posted 2026/07/21 , listed until 2027/01/21) Position Description: Apply Position Description The International Comparative Studies Program (ICS), Duke University is seeking an experienced Research Associate for a short-term project-based contract.  They will gather data and research on the impact that ICS has at Duke and where it stands in relation to similar critical global studies programs around the country. Tasks will include:\r\n1.\tTabulating data over time on ICS majors, the courses they take, and who teaches them, their other majors, and certificates. \r\n2.\tComparing ICS student engagement with other interpretive social sciences and humanities major students at Duke.\r\n3.\tDrawing a picture of who our Gateway students have been over the last ten years\r\n4.\tDescribing and analyzing the kinds of research our seniors do – language, themes, countries, disciplines, etc. Successful candidates will understand the undergraduate landscape at Duke, have an excellent grasp of the goals and culture of the Comparative International Studies program, and be willing to engage with student-facing software at Duke. We expect the completion of the work in December 2026. This position requires a PhD. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Further Info: https://internationalcomparative.duke.edu/ 1304 campus dr box 90405 durham nc 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
