    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32902": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Duke University, Mechanical Engineering and Materials Science Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Mechanical Engineering and Materials Science Position ID: Duke-MEMS-PDZHONG2 [#32902] Position Title: Postdoctoral Associate Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27701, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Engineering / Materials Science , Materials Science and Engineering , Mechanical Engineering Appl Deadline: 2026/11/30 23:59:59  (posted 2026/10/01 , listed until 2026/11/30) Position Description: Apply Position Description Duke University was created in 1924 through an indenture of\r\ntrust by James Buchanan Duke. Today, Duke is regarded as one of America’s\r\nleading research universities. Located in Durham, North Carolina, Duke is\r\npositioned in the heart of the Research Triangle, which is ranked annually as\r\none of the best places in the country to work and live. Duke has more than\r\n15,000 students who study and conduct research in its 10 undergraduate,\r\ngraduate and professional schools. With about 40,000 employees, Duke is the third\r\nlargest private employer in North Carolina, and it now has international\r\nprograms in more than 150 countries. The Pratt School of Engineering at Duke University is one\r\nof the fastest rising, highly ranked engineering schools in the nation. The\r\nschool consists of four departments with 130 tenure-track faculty members, 1250\r\nundergraduate students, 1400 master’s students, and 600 PhD students. Housed\r\nwithin a university renowned for its programs in the liberal arts, medicine,\r\nbusiness and law, Pratt prepares future engineering leaders to address complex\r\nchallenges with solutions that take into account the complexities and nuances\r\nof implementing them. Pratt takes a whole-student approach to education,\r\nensuring students are well supported throughout their journeys and graduate\r\nwith the grit and determination required to persevere in their careers and\r\ntheir lives. Pratt is dedicated to the values of diversity, equitability,\r\ninclusivity and community in its pursuit of knowledge and service to society. Occupational Summary Conduct independent research activities under the\r\nguidance of a faculty mentor in preparation for a full-time academic or\r\nresearch career. Work Performed DEFINITION: The Postdoctoral Appointee holds a Ph.D. or equivalent\r\ndoctorate (e.g. ScD, MD, DVM). Candidates with non-US degrees may be required\r\nto provide proof of degree equivalency. 1. A candidate may also be appointed to a postdoctoral\r\nposition if the candidate has completed all the requirements for a degree, but\r\nthe degree has not been formally conferred: in this case, the candidate may\r\npresent evidence of completion of the degree requirements, together with a\r\nstatement documenting the date on which the degree is to be conferred. If the\r\ndegree is not conferred by this projected date, the postdoctoral appointment\r\nmay be terminated. 2. Note for international candidates: Generally,\r\nimmigration classifications (e.g., H-1B, J-1, etc.) require that the requisite\r\ndegree be conferred before a petition can be filed or a visa document issued to\r\nsponsor the individual. The term of the appointment is limited (see Section 5\r\nof the Postdoc Policy for the length of the appointment). The appointment involves substantially full-time\r\nresearch or scholarship and may include teaching responsibilities. The Postdoctoral Appointee functions under the\r\nsupervision of a mentor or a department at Duke University. The Postdoctoral Appointee is expected to publish the\r\nresults of his or her research or scholarship during the period of the\r\nappointment. EXPECTATION: The conscientious discharge of research or scholarship\r\nresponsibilities, which may include teaching responsibilities for Postdoctoral\r\nAssociates Conformance to standards of responsible conduct in\r\nresearch Compliance with good scholarly and research\r\npractice Maintenance of a laboratory notebook and/or other\r\ncomparable records of research activity, which remains the property of Duke\r\nUniversity upon termination Adherence to University standards regarding the use of\r\nchemicals and the like Open and timely discussion with the mentor regarding\r\nall facets of the Postdoctoral Appointee's research activities. Postdoctoral\r\nAppointees are encouraged to consult the AAMC Compact Between Postdoctoral\r\nAppointees and Their Mentors for suggested guidelines for the Postdoctoral\r\nAppointee-mentor relationship Prompt disclosure to the mentor regarding the\r\npossession and desire to distribute materials, reagents, software, and\r\ncopyrightable and potentially patentable discoveries derived from the\r\nPostdoctoral Appointee's research. Collegial conduct towards members of the research group\r\nand others as described in the Duke University School of Medicine Honor Code of\r\nProfessional Conduct and other relevant conduct policies about other schools at\r\nDuke University. Compliance with all applicable University and\r\ndepartmental policies and procedures. The postdoc candidate will be responsible for laser\r\ninstrumentation design, integration, and testing, perform\r\nexperimental and model simulations for laser-induced bubble\r\ndynamics, temperature change, and material damage assessment; develop\r\nand validate AI and machine learning models for robot-assisted laser surgery. Minimum Qualifications The candidate should have a Ph.D. in Engineering, Physics,\r\nApplied Mathematics, Computer Science, or a related area. Experience 0+ years of postgraduate experience. Skills The candidate must have excellent skills in conducting independent research\r\nand collaboration within a vibrant multidisciplinary team of mechanical and\r\nbiomedical engineers, material scientists, computational modeling experts, and\r\nclinicians. Excellent communication and\r\ninterpersonal skills, including formal writing skills are essential . ________________________________________________________________________________________ This job description intends to provide a\r\nrepresentative and level of the types of duties and responsibilities that will\r\nbe required of positions given this title and shall not be construed as a\r\ndeclaration of the total of the specific duties and responsibilities of any\r\nparticular position. Employees may be directed to perform job-related tasks\r\nother than those specifically presented in this description. Duke is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer\r\ncommitted to providing employment opportunity without regard to an individual's\r\nage, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic\r\ninformation, national origin, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or\r\nveteran status. Duke aspires to create a community built on collaboration,\r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on the\r\nrobust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich diversity of\r\nour perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To achieve this\r\nexchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel secure and\r\nwelcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all\r\nvoices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility to uphold\r\nthese values. Essential Physical Job Functions: Certain jobs at Duke University and Duke University Health\r\nSystem may include essential job functions that require specific physical\r\nand/or mental abilities. Additional information and provision for requests for\r\nreasonable accommodation will be provided by each hiring department. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.mems.duke.edu 919-660-5310 Duke University Thomas Lord Department of Mechanical Engineering & Materials Science Box 90300 Durham, NC  27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32871": {
      "title": "Part-time Instructor",
      "description": "Duke University, Music Department Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Music Department Position ID: Duke-Music Department-NRRVIOLA [#32871] Position Title: Part-time Instructor Position Type: Non-regular rank faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Music / Viola Appl Deadline: 2026/10/30 23:59:59 * (posted 2026/10/01 , updated 2026/09/29 ) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Department of Music at Duke University seeks to hire an instructor to teach applied viola to music majors, minors, and non-majors. The position entails a teaching load of up to 8 hours per week, along with participation in additional string area activities for one hour per week. This is a one semester non-regular rank leave replacement faculty position for the spring 2027 semester. Primary Responsibilities: • Applied Lessons: provide weekly viola instruction to undergraduate and graduate students. •\tChamber Music Coaching: coaching of up to three chamber music ensembles as part of the 8 hour teaching load. •\tString Area Events: attend and support all string area activities, including: o\tviola area recitals o\tviola/string/chamber music master classes o\tstudent recitals o 1-2 studio classes The instructor will be expected to ensure continuity and a smooth transition for existing viola students. A high level of professionalism, reliability, and integration into the department’s curriculum is essential. A master’s degree or higher in viola performance is required, as well as demonstrated excellence in viola performance and broad knowledge of the repertoire. In addition, candidates must have collegiate-level teaching experience, including individual instruction and chamber music coaching. Please submit a CV and cover letter by October 30, 2026. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: music.duke.edu 919-660-3310 Duke University Department of Music PO Box 90665 Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32697": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor - Neurobiology",
      "description": "Duke University, Neurobiology Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Neurobiology Position ID: Duke-Neuro-COMPUTATIONALNEUROBIOLOGY [#32697] Position Title: Tenure Track Assistant Professor - Neurobiology Position Type: Tenured/Tenure-track faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27710-0001, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Neuroscience / Computational neuroscience Appl Deadline: 2026/12/01 23:59:59  (posted 2026/09/15 , listed until 2027/03/15) Position Description: Apply Position Description Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Computational or Theoretical Neuroscience. The Department of Neurobiology at Duke University School of Medicine invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in Computational or Theoretical Neuroscience at the Assistant Professor level. We seek outstanding scientists who use computational or theoretical approaches to address fundamental questions in neuroscience. Research areas are broadly defined and may include models of neural circuits and population dynamics; computational and theoretical studies of brain development, organization, and function; models of perception, cognition, learning, and behavior; and statistical and machine-learning approaches for understanding neural data and brain function. We are particularly interested in candidates who will establish an ambitious, externally-funded, independent research program and build collaborations with experimental groups across Duke’s neuroscience community. This position offers an opportunity to help shape and expand computational and theoretical neuroscience at Duke within a highly interactive neuroscience environment. The successful candidate will have opportunities for collaboration within Neurobiology and across Duke, including Biomedical Engineering, Neurosurgery, Psychology and Neuroscience, Computer Science, and the Duke Institute for Brain Sciences, as well as connections to Duke initiatives in AI and data science. This search is distinct from, but complementary to, the Duke University Discovery AI Initiative https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32313 , which also includes a position associated with the Department of Neurobiology. Applicants should submit a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, a research strategy (3 page maximum including figures and references) describing major research accomplishments and plans for future research, a one page statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy, up to 3 significant publications each with short statement of significance, and three letters of recommendation.  Teaching opportunities for this position will be primarily at the graduate level. All application materials should be submitted electronically to Academic Jobs Online https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/Duke/Neuro/COMPUTATIONALNEUROBIOLOGY . Questions regarding the application process may be addressed to samuel.kamau@duke.edu. Applications will be reviewed as they are received until the position is filled, with priority given to applications received by December 1, 2026. Duke University is an equal opportunity employer. Please note that Duke specifically seeks information about past misconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or relevant pending investigations as part of the application process. Disclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will initiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to address them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees during the application review process. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Three publications Statement of Teaching and Mentoring philosophy, Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site. Letters should be submitted by 2026/12/01 11:59PM ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.neuro.duke.edu 9196841568 Box 3209 Duke University Medical Center Durham, NC 27710-0001 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32890": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate - Marine Geospatial Ecology Lab",
      "description": "Duke University, NSOE-ESS & MSC Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, NSOE-ESS & MSC Position ID: Duke-NSOE-ESS & MSC-POSTDOC_HALPIN [#32890] Position Title: Postdoctoral Associate - Marine Geospatial Ecology Lab Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Beaufort, North Carolina 28516, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Statistics / Data Processing & Modeling Marine Science / Marine Ecology Marine Biology / Biology Oceanography / Oceanography Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/09/30 , listed until 2026/11/01) Position Description: Apply Position Description Submit the following required materials at the\r\nAcademic Jobs Online posting (Posting Number 32890) to apply for this position. Curriculum vitae Cover letter Name and contact information for at least three professional\r\n     references. References will be contacted only at a later stage of the process. Occupational Summary The Marine Geospatial Ecology Lab\r\n(MGEL) at Duke University seeks a Postdoctoral\r\nAssociate or Research Scientist (depending on level of experience) in marine\r\nspatial ecology for immediate hire to model species distributions of marine\r\nmammals. Directed by Dr. Patrick Halpin, MGEL is a leader in applying\r\ngeospatial analysis to problems in marine ecology, wildlife management, and\r\nocean conservation. We are seeking a highly motivated individual who is\r\ninterested in collaborative modeling of marine mammal species distributions,\r\nwith a focus on developing management-ready results and accompanying\r\nmanuscripts suitable for immediate application to ongoing protected species\r\nmanagement problems and environmental planning activities in the United States.\r\nThis is a two-year position, with extension beyond that contingent upon\r\nevaluation and funding.  The position may\r\nbe located on-site at Duke University in Durham, NC, at the Duke University\r\nMarine Lab in Beaufort, NC, or off-site/remote as permitted under university policy.\r\nSalary and job title commensurate with degree/experience. The successful\r\ncandidate will join a small team that models spatiotemporal distributions of\r\nmarine species and applies the results to management problems throughout the\r\nworld. The work will involve bouts of collaborative research and problem\r\nsolving with other team members interspersed with periods of extensive\r\nindependent, candidate-led modeling and development. The candidate will be\r\nexpected to travel to scientific conferences and meetings to present and\r\ndiscuss results of the research, to keep abreast of developments in the field,\r\nand to author and submit publications to peer-reviewed scientific journals.\r\nThis position is best suited to those who enjoy statistics, coding,\r\nmanipulating and visualizing data, and other quantitative analysis tasks. Work Performed The new\r\nresearcher will leverage knowledge and skills from a number of fields,\r\nincluding marine mammal ecology and biology, statistical modeling,\r\noceanography, and scientific programming and data processing to model species\r\ndistributions for several motivating management applications. The taxonomic\r\nfocus will be marine mammals, particularly cetaceans, with a special emphasis\r\non the North Atlantic right whale. The primary task will be to develop density\r\nsurface models from visual line transect surveys conducted by a large number of\r\ncollaborating institutions along with gridded environmental covariates derived\r\nfrom satellite remote sensing, ocean models, and distribution models of prey\r\nspecies. Specific primary\r\ntasks include: cleaning and integrating line transect surveys into a common\r\ndatabase; modeling species detection probability with distance sampling;\r\nselection, acquiring, reformatting, and sampling gridded environmental data for\r\nuse as model covariates; creating species density surface models, typically in\r\nR with mgcv-based generalized additive models; predicting density and\r\nuncertainty surfaces from those models; evaluating predictions with\r\ncontemporaneous data not included in the models as well as knowledge from the\r\nliterature; and communicating results effectively in presentations and\r\nmanuscripts. All projects will require regular interaction with internal and\r\nexternal collaborators and funders. The candidate must be comfortable and effective\r\nin leading discussions about project status and current results in audio and\r\nvideo calls and in-person meetings, and communicating the same in formal and\r\ninformal written reports. The ideal\r\ncandidate will be able to carefully reason about what inference can be gained\r\nthrough the joining and modeling of diverse datasets. This will include\r\ninvestigating how well models may be transferred to unsurveyed areas, seasons,\r\nor conditions and designing models to maximize transferability. The researcher\r\nmust be comfortable working with imperfect sampling designs and heterogeneous\r\ncoverage, and be prepared to deal with strong spatial and temporal biases in\r\nsurvey effort. Some degree of extrapolation will almost always be required, and\r\nthe researcher must decide what is acceptable and be prepared to defend their\r\nchoices with evidence. Specific projects\r\ndepend on MGEL team needs and researcher experience but are likely to include: Projecting Changes to North\r\n     Atlantic Right Whale Density in the 21st Century and the Future Risk of\r\n     Overlap with U.S. Lobster Fisheries. A\r\n     persistent threat to the endangered North Atlantic right whale ( Eubalaena\r\n     glacialis ) is entanglement in buoy lines in trap and pot fisheries,\r\n     such as those for American lobster ( Homarus americanus ). In the\r\n     United States, the densest aggregations of lobster buoy lines occur in the\r\n     Gulf of Maine, which is also an important feeding area for the right\r\n     whale. The rapid warming of the Gulf of Maine has been linked to\r\n     distribution shifts in both species. For this project, the researcher will\r\n     collaborate with a multi-institution research team that is investigating\r\n     how continued warming might drive further changes in upcoming decades, and\r\n     what effects this might have on entanglement risk in the future. The new\r\n     researcher’s primary role will be to lead development and publication of a\r\n     model that forecasts future right whale density distribution from ocean\r\n     climate models, and then collaborate with the wider team on models of\r\n     future whale, lobster, and fishery overlap. Marine Mammal Density Models for\r\n     the U.S. Navy Atlantic Fleet Testing and Training Study Area. Under the U.S. Marine Mammal Protection Act (MMPA), the U.S.\r\n     Navy is responsible for assessing potential impacts of peacetime testing\r\n     and training activities to marine mammal populations every seven years. To\r\n     facilitate this, as well as other environmental planning processes, the\r\n     Navy initiated a cooperative research agreement with MGEL to develop new\r\n     density surface models for all marine mammal species along the east and\r\n     Gulf coasts of North America, as well as a wider study area that extends\r\n     east to longitude 45 °W . For this project, the researcher will lead the development\r\n     of a portion of the full suite of models developed for the Navy’s needs,\r\n     collaborating closely with other MGEL staff who are similarly working on a\r\n     different package of models. While project #1 will focus on a single\r\n     high-priority species in a forecasting scenario, project #2 will focus on\r\n     a larger number of species in a more traditional hindcasting scenario,\r\n     with an emphasis on obtaining a set of consistent, high-quality models\r\n     that are equally suitable for subsequent population impact analysis under\r\n     the MMPA by the Navy and other interested parties. This project will\r\n     culminate with release of the models to the Navy as well as to the public\r\n     online, with opportunities for the new researcher to lead or coauthor\r\n     resulting publications. Advanced Models and Modeling\r\n     Methods. While progressing on core\r\n     modeling projects such as those above, the new researcher may also\r\n     participate in ongoing and upcoming projects that involve less developed,\r\n     more experimental models and modeling methodologies. These may include:\r\n     methods for integrating visual and passive acoustic surveys into a single\r\n     model; methods for developing and actual development of density models\r\n     that span the entire North Atlantic basin, addressing the problems of\r\n     disparate survey data, geographically-differing species-habitat\r\n     relationships, and large unsurveyed expanses that require substantial\r\n     extrapolation; and a system that issues nowcasts and short-term density\r\n     forecasts for North Atlantic right whale in near real-time. Term One year initial appointment with potential for annual renewal for a total two-year term with extension beyond that contingent upon\r\nevaluation and funding. Work Location The position may\r\nbe located at Duke University in Durham, NC, the Duke University Marine Lab in\r\nBeaufort, NC, or off-site/remote as permitted under university policy. Salary\r\nand job title commensurate with degree/experience. Required Qualifications at this level Education / Training Academic\r\ncredentials: PhD in ecology, biology, statistics, computer science or\r\nengineering, or oceanography with a strong quantitative analysis background,\r\nparticularly in species distribution modeling. Specific coursework and research\r\nexperience with marine mammals strongly preferred. Undergraduate coursework or\r\nequivalent experience in physical and biological oceanography is required;\r\ngraduate-level coursework or research experience preferred. Experience This position requires a minimum of two years of direct work experience,\r\nor academic equivalent. Skills We are seeking\r\ncandidates with both a mix of established skills and a strong aptitude to learn\r\nnew skills. Communication · Fluent in English (reading, writing,\r\nand speaking). · Proficient in giving scientific\r\npresentations, with demonstrated experience. · Strong scientific writing skills, in\r\nEnglish; record of peer-reviewed publications preferred. Teamwork · Competent working in a team on joint\r\nprojects, both face-to-face and remotely. · Also able to work independently, with\r\nonly occasional oversight. · Comfortable and competent interacting\r\nwith external collaborators, funders, and stakeholders. · Capable of working on multiple\r\nprojects simultaneously. · Able to complete tasks on time and\r\nmeet hard deadlines imposed by funders, regulatory processes, or other external\r\nsources. Analysis and Modeling · Strong background and competency in\r\nmathematics (undergraduate calculus, at minimum). Graduate-level training or\r\nexperience is a plus. · Strong proficiency in statistical\r\nmodeling, including regression and classification modeling. Experience with\r\nGLMs and GAMs strongly preferred. · Demonstrated experience with species\r\ndistribution modeling and/or abundance estimation, including habitat\r\nsuitability modeling, distance sampling, occupancy modeling, capture-recapture\r\nmethods, etc. Specific experience with distance sampling and density surface\r\nmodeling strongly preferred. · Strong programming skills, including\r\nproficiency in R (or equivalent with ability to quickly become proficient in\r\nR). Proficiency with Python, MATLAB, and SQL a plus. · Demonstrated competence with GIS,\r\ngeospatial analysis, and mapping. Ability to automate production of maps and\r\nother geospatial operations preferred. · Competent at working with multiple\r\ntabular data formats, including CSV and other text formats, and relational\r\ndatabases (MS Access, SQL, etc.). · Proficient in summarizing/aggregating,\r\ntransforming, and joining tabular data in R and/or SQL. · Proficiency utilizing large language\r\nmodels effectively to aid research is a plus. Experience with agentic\r\nprogramming is a plus. However, these and other AI technologies are no\r\nsubstitute for unassisted skill, logical thinking, attention to detail, scientific\r\ndiscipline, deep understanding, precise communication, and original creativity. Additional Information: Duke University is an Equal Opportunity\r\nEmployer committed to providing employment opportunity without regard to an\r\nindividual's age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender\r\nidentity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, (including\r\npregnancy and pregnancy related conditions), sexual orientation, or military\r\nstatus. Duke aspires to create a community built on\r\ncollaboration, innovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success\r\ndepends on the robust exchange of ideas-an exchange that is best when the rich\r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To\r\nachieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel\r\nsecure and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected,\r\nand that all voices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility\r\nto uphold these values. Essential Physical Job Functions Certain jobs at Duke\r\nUniversity and Duke University Health System may include essential job\r\nfunctions that require specific physical and/or mental abilities. Additional\r\ninformation and provision for requests for reasonable accommodation will be\r\nprovided by each hiring department. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://nicholas.duke.edu/ 2525047538 Grainger Hall 9 Circuit Drive, Box 90328 Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32707": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate (Duke Critical Minerals Hub)",
      "description": "Duke University, NSOE-ESS & MSC Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, NSOE-ESS & MSC Position ID: Duke-NSOE-ESS & MSC-POSTDOC_WEINTHAL [#32707] Position Title: Postdoctoral Associate (Duke Critical Minerals Hub) Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Environmental Studies Environmental Economics, Policy, and Science Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/09/15 , listed until 2026/10/31) Position Description: Apply Position Description Occupational Summary: The Critical Minerals Hub at Duke invites applications for a full-time postdoctoral associate to conduct cutting-edge research in critical minerals governance. This position will contribute to building interdisciplinary excellence and research pathways in critical minerals research at Duke University. The successful candidate will report to and work with faculty affiliated with the policy research taskforce of the Critical Minerals Hub at Duke University and will support collaboration and integration at the nexus of multiple disciplines involved in the Hub. The successful applicant will possess a PhD or equivalent doctoral degree in the social sciences, including political science, public policy, political ecology, law, geography or another relevant field with some research experience related to critical minerals. Relevant research areas include critical minerals governance and law; governing critical minerals supply chains from the local to the transnational and global scales; national security and critical minerals governance; principles and norms for mitigating environmental and social impacts from mining; and institutional design and practices for addressing benefits-sharing. In addition to conducting an original, primary research project, the successful candidate will be expected to engage in collaborative research, mentoring (graduate and undergraduate students), and education activities related to the Critical Minerals Hub. The postdoctoral associate will gain valuable insights into center-level research and education activities, support in seeking funding for research, and the opportunity to build grant writing and proposal development skills, engage with diverse external partners in academia and industry, and gain mentorship from multiple faculty across disciplines. Work performed: The primary duties of this position include: Research Activities (75%) •\tCo-design and conducting both original applied and fundamental social science research involving critical minerals governance, law, and policy. Potential project topics are diverse and should be co-developed, drawing on the diverse expertise of Hub faculty; •\tAssisting in developing research proposals for external funding agencies; Programmatic Support (25%) •\tCo-leading, participating in, and supporting Hub activities, including a Bass Connections project; •\tMentoring students and trainees involve in Hub activities; Term: The initial appointment will be for one year with the possibility for extension.  Salary will be commensurate with experience and postdoctoral associate standards. Projected Start Date: January to July 2027 Work location: Durham, NC – Nicholas School of the Environment. Required Qualifications at this Level: Education/Training Work requires a PhD or equivalent doctoral degree in the social sciences, including science, public policy, political ecology, law, geography, or another relevant field with some research experience related to critical minerals. Relevant research areas include critical minerals governance and law; governing critical minerals supply chains from the local to the transnational and global scales; national security and critical minerals governance; and principles and norms for mitigating environmental and social impacts from mining and for promoting local benefits. The successful applicant will have training in both qualitative and quantitative methods, including field research. Experience Work requires a PhD or equivalent doctoral degree Skills Preferred Qualifications (if applicable) . Successful candidates must be able to communicate the applicability of their prior research experiences to the critical minerals thematic area of the Hub and describe the interdisciplinary importance of these research experiences. Applicants should have an excellent academic record and thrive in a collaborative multidisciplinary research environment. A background and demonstrated experience working at the intersection of disciplines is desired; expressed interest in working at this intersection is required. The candidate should have the ability to work in a diverse lab group setting, planning and conducting assessments independently and collaboratively, interpreting data, and presenting results to diverse audiences. Fluency in English (written and oral) is required. Excellent scientific writing skills and the ability to organize data and clearly disseminate findings and scientific concepts are essential. In addition to applicants’ prior accomplishments, we would like to learn what motivates them, how they approach unfamiliar problems, and what they hope to contribute to, and learn from, an interdisciplinary research community. Additional Information: The initial review of applications will begin approximately October 5, 2026 and continue on a rolling basis until the position is filled. In addition to applicants’ prior accomplishments, we would like to learn what motivates them, how they approach unfamiliar problems, and what they hope to contribute to, and learn from, an interdisciplinary research community. Applications should include: •\tCurriculum vitae. •\tCover letter, up to two pages. Please introduce yourself and explain why this particular postdoctoral opportunity interests you. In addition to describing your relevant experience, address: o\tWhat draws you to critical minerals and materials as a research area; o\tWhat perspective, expertise, or way of thinking you would bring to the CM Hub @Duke; o\tAn example of a time you worked across disciplinary boundaries, entered an unfamiliar area, \r\nor helped connect people or ideas that are not usually brought together; o\tSomething about your interests, motivations, or working style that we would not learn from your CV. •\tOne-page career-development statement. Describe the career you hope to build, the capabilities or experiences you would like to develop during the postdoctoral appointment, and how this position could help you move toward those goals. We encourage applicants to identify what a successful postdoctoral experience would look like for them. •\tOne-page research vision. Identify one or two research questions or opportunities related to critical minerals and materials that you find especially compelling. We are particularly interested in ideas that connect disciplines, methods, or parts of the critical-minerals system. Briefly explain why the question matters, how you might begin approaching it, and what expertise or collaborations would strengthen the work. These ideas are intended to help us understand your creativity and research instincts; they will not be treated as a fixed proposal or commitment. •\tNames and contact information for at least three professional references. References will be contacted only at a later stage of the search. Applicants are welcome to use figures, conceptual diagrams, or other concise visual elements in their statements when these help communicate their ideas. For questions pertaining to this position, please send an email to criticalminerals@duke.edu and someone from our leadership team will respond. General Information The intent of this job description is to provide a representative and level of the types of duties and responsibilities that will be required of positions given this title and shall not be construed as a declaration of the total of the specific duties and responsibilities of any particular position. Employees may be directed to perform job-related tasks other than those specifically presented in this description. Duke University is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment opportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, (including pregnancy and pregnancy related conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke aspires to create a community built on collaboration, innovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on the robust exchange of ideas-an exchange that is best when the rich diversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility to uphold these values.\r\n \r\nEssential Physical Job Functions Certain jobs at Duke University and Duke University Health System may include essential job functions that require specific physical and/or mental abilities. Additional information and provision for requests for reasonable accommodation will be provided by each hiring department. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae One-Page Career Development Statement One-Page Research Vision Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://criticalminerals.duke.edu/ Grainger Hall 9 Circuit Drive, Box 90328 Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32821": {
      "title": "Eads Professor of the Practice in Energy",
      "description": "Duke University, NSOE-Faculty Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, NSOE-Faculty Position ID: Duke-NSOEFaculty-EADS [#32821] Position Title: Eads Professor of the Practice in Energy Position Type: Non tenure-track faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Energy / Economics , Finance , Policy Energy Systems Energy Engineering Energy systems engineering Appl Deadline: 2026/11/01 23:59:59  (posted 2026/09/24 , listed until 2027/03/24) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Nicholas School of the Environment at Duke University invites applications for the Eads\r\nProfessor of the Practice in Energy (open rank: Assistant, Associate, Full). We seek an\r\noutstanding teacher and practitioner with expertise in energy systems and their interactions with the\r\nenvironment, economy, and society. The successful candidate will contribute to the Nicholas School’s teaching and educational programs\r\nin energy, with particular emphasis on preparing students to understand and address the rapidly\r\nevolving challenges facing energy systems. The candidate will also be affiliated with the Nicholas\r\nInstitute for Energy, Environment and Sustainability and engage in activities with decision-makers in\r\nthe public and private sector. While we are particularly interested in energy finance experience, we\r\nwelcome candidates from a broad range of disciplinary backgrounds, including economics, finance,\r\nbusiness, public policy, engineering, environmental science, or related fields, as well as from a range\r\nof energy sector experiences.\r\nWe will consider applications from those completing or recently completing a PhD or other terminal\r\ndegree, if they have industry, government, consulting, nonprofit, or other professional experience,\r\nand demonstrate exceptional promise as teachers and contributors to Duke’s energy community. Teaching and Advising Responsibilities The Eads Professor of the Practice will teach and advise students in the Nicholas School’s\r\nprofessional master’s and undergraduate programs and may contribute to other Duke educational\r\nprograms related to energy. Teaching, advising, and service will account for approximately\r\nthree-quarters of the position’s responsibilities and will include a course load equivalent to 3.5\r\nsemester-long courses per academic year, advising master’s projects and independent studies, and\r\nmentoring students pursuing careers in energy. The remaining one-quarter will be devoted to\r\nprofessional practice aligned with the Nicholas Institute for Energy, Environment & Sustainability. A central responsibility of the position will be teaching a course on Energy Finance. This course\r\nshould provide students with the concepts and analytical tools needed to understand and evaluate\r\nfinancial decisions and investments in the energy sector. Depending on the candidate’s expertise and the needs of the School, the successful candidate may\r\nalso develop and teach courses in areas such as: ●\r\nEnergy economics ●\r\nMicrogrids and distributed energy systems ●\r\nRenewable energy and energy storage ●\r\nEnergy infrastructure and project development ●\r\nEnergy policy and regulation ●\r\nCorporate energy and decarbonization strategies ●\r\nEmerging energy technologies and business models ●\r\nArtificial intelligence, data centers, and energy systems ●\r\nPower Systems planning, operations, and markets We are particularly interested in candidates who can connect fundamental concepts with practical\r\ndecision-making and expose students to the tradeoffs involved in real-world energy decisions. Practice Beyond formal teaching, the successful candidate will contribute to the intellectual and professional\r\ncommunity surrounding energy at Duke through their appointment at the Nicholas Institute for\r\nEnergy, Environment and Sustainability. This may include engaging with energy companies, electric\r\npower utilities, financial institutions, government agencies, nonprofit organizations, research\r\ninstitutions, and other external partners; bringing practitioners and experts to campus; developing\r\nexperiential learning opportunities; and helping students connect their academic training with\r\nemerging energy challenges and career opportunities. We welcome different forms of contribution to this community. More experienced candidates may\r\nbring extensive professional networks, leadership experience, or sector expertise, while early-career\r\ncandidates may bring emerging areas of expertise, new analytical approaches, innovative teaching\r\nideas, or experience with rapidly developing energy technologies and markets. Duke University, the Nicholas School, and the Nicholas Institute for Energy, Environment &\r\nSustainability provide an especially rich environment for interdisciplinary work on energy. The\r\nsuccessful candidate will have opportunities to collaborate with faculty and programs across Duke\r\nworking in energy systems, climate change, environmental science, sustainability, engineering,\r\neconomics, public policy, business, law, data science, and related fields, as well as through Duke’s\r\nuniversity-wide climate and sustainability initiatives. Candidates should have: ● A PhD or terminal degree in economics, finance, business, public policy, environmental science, engineering, management, law, or another field relevant to energy. Demonstrated expertise in energy through professional, academic,  or applied work. ● A strong interest in interdisciplinary energy education. ● Evidence of excellence in teaching or strong potential to become an excellent teacher. ● The ability to communicate complex technical, economic, financial, environmental, or policy concepts effectively to students from a variety of disciplinary backgrounds. ● An interest in connecting classroom learning with real-world energy challenges. Application Requirements Applications can be submitted online at https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo job # 32821 and must include: ● A cover letter describing the applicant’s interest in the position and how their background would contribute to energy education at the Nicholas School; ● A current curriculum vitae; ● A statement describing the applicant’s approach to teaching and mentoring, including courses they would be interested in teaching or developing at Duke; ● Evidence of teaching effectiveness, if available ● A statement describing the applicant’s professional practice; ● Three representative examples of professional practice, scholarship, or other relevant work; and ● A list of three references who may be contacted later in the search process. Review of applications will begin November 1, and applications received by that date will be given full consideration. We will continue reviewing applications until the position is filled. The anticipated start date is August 1, 2027. Questions regarding the position may be directed to EadsEnergySearch@duke.edu About the Nicholas School of the Environment The Nicholas School of the Environment is committed to developing knowledge and leaders capable of addressing the world’s most pressing environmental challenges. Its faculty span the natural sciences, social sciences, engineering, economics, policy, finance, and management, creating a highly interdisciplinary environment for research and education. The position will be based on Duke University’s main campus in Durham, North Carolina. Duke is a world-class research university with extensive opportunities for collaboration across the Nicholas School, the Nicholas Institute for Energy, Environment and Sustainability, Fuqua School of Business, Pratt School of Engineering, Sanford School of Public Policy, Duke Law School, and other schools, institutes, and programs. About the Nicholas Institute The Nicholas Institute for Energy, Environment & Sustainability at Duke University accelerates solutions to critical energy and environmental challenges, advancing a more just, resilient, and sustainable world. The Institute conducts and supports actionable research and undertakes sustained engagement with policymakers, businesses, and communities, in addition to delivering transformative educational experiences to empower future leaders. Equal Employment Opportunity Duke is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment opportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy-related conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke aspires to create a community built on collaboration, innovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich diversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Reference Checks Please note that Duke specifically seeks information about past misconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or relevant pending investigations as part of the application process. Disclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will initiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to address them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees during the application review process. Salary and benefits Salary will be commensurate with experience, qualifications, and rank. The base annual salary corresponds to a nine-month academic-year appointment and can be supplemented with additional summer salary from grants and other sources.  Duke University also offers a comprehensive benefits package; additional information is available through Duke Human Resources — Benefits at Duke . Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Curriculum Vitae Three representative scholarship/practice samples A statement describing the applicant’s approach to teaching and mentoring, including courses they would be interested in teaching or develop Evidence of teaching effectiveness, if available A statement describing the applicant’s professional practice A  cover letter describing the applicant’s interest in the position and how their background would contribute to energy education at the Nic And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.env.duke.edu Box 90329 Duke University Durham NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32617": {
      "title": "Energy Geoscience",
      "description": "Duke University, NSOE-Faculty Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, NSOE-Faculty Position ID: Duke-NSOEFaculty-EE [#32617] Position Title: Energy Geoscience Position Type: Tenured/Tenure-track faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Energy / Critical Minerals , Energy and Environment Renewable Energy / Nuclear Waste Disposal , geo-based synthesis of next-generation fuels Geothermal Energy Geosciences / Geologic Carbon Storage Earth and Environmental Sciences / Earth science Appl Deadline: 2026/12/31 23:59:59 * (posted 2026/09/04 ) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Division of Earth and Climate Sciences (ECS) in the Nicholas School of the Environment at Duke University invites applicants for a tenure-track position as Assistant Professor in Energy Geoscience . ECS plans to hire an Earth Scientist whose research focuses on Energy. We seek an innovative scholar who will develop a thriving research program using geoscience methods to help advance ongoing transitions in energy production and management, such as geothermal energy, exploration for critical minerals used in energy technologies, geo-based synthesis of next-generation fuels like hydrogen, geologic carbon storage, underground disposal of nuclear waste, and other themes at the energy-environment nexus, including those that relate to climate change. Faculty within ECS benefit from close interdisciplinary collaboration with social science, health science, and environmental science faculty within the Nicholas School and other schools at Duke. Duke University is a world class research institution that prides itself on interdisciplinary scholarship, teaching and service.  Science, policy, and solutions-oriented collaborations with programs outside of the Nicholas School including Engineering, Public Policy or Medicine are encouraged. Teaching and Advising Responsibilities : The successful candidate will be responsible for teaching the equivalent of 2.5 semester-long courses each academic year across a portfolio of fundamental and applied energy and energy and the environment topics. The ability to develop new classes in the research areas outlined above will make an important contribution to NSOE’s vibrant Energy and Environment professional master’s program and ECS’s undergraduate major. The successful candidate will also advise PhD students, professional student Master’s Projects, and undergraduate research. Location : The position will be located on the main campus of Duke University, fostering direct interactions between the faculty and students. Duke University also maintains the Duke Forest research forest as well as the Duke University Marine Lab. Duke aspires to create a community built on collaboration, innovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich diversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Review of applications will begin October 31, 2026 and continue through December 31, 2026, when the application window closes. Interested candidates should submit the following: a cover letter (with contact information); a curriculum vitae; a three-page research statement that includes a description of past research accomplishments, plans for future research, and their experience and approach to mentoring and supporting  a diverse and inclusive research environment; a one-page statement describing their teaching philosophy, previous experiences, future plans for teaching, and experience and approach to fostering an inclusive learning and mentoring environment for their students; and three reference letters. Qualifications: PhD or equivalent in relevant field. Duke is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment opportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy related conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Please note that Duke specifically seeks information about past misconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or relevant pending investigations as part of the application process. Disclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will initiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to address them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees during the application review process. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae three-page research statement that includes a description of past research accomplishments, plans for future research, and their experience one-page statement describing their teaching philosophy, previous experiences, future plans for teaching, and experience and approach to fos Three reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.env.duke.edu Box 90329 Duke University Durham NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32513": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor in Environmental Health",
      "description": "Duke University, NSOE-Faculty Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, NSOE-Faculty Position ID: Duke-NSOEFaculty-EH [#32513] Position Title: Assistant or Associate Professor in Environmental Health Position Type: Tenured/Tenure-track faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Environmental Health / Environmental Health , Environmental Toxicology Environmental / Epigenetics Computational Toxicology Climate and Health Developmental Toxicology Appl Deadline: 2026/10/23 23:59:59 Position Description: Apply Position Description Anticipated start: July 1, 2027 The Nicholas School of the Environment invites applications for a tenure-track position in Environmental Health with an emphasis on toxicology and pollutant effects. The successful candidate will participate in and contribute to the T32-supported Integrated Toxicology and Environmental Health Program . We seek applicants at the Assistant or Associate level who will establish widely recognized, externally funded research programs. Wet or dry lab programs are both of interest. Research programs focused on computational toxicology, environmental epigenetics, climate and toxicology, and developmental toxicology are of particular interest, as is the ability to teach toxicology, risk assessment, and/or statistics. We seek a researcher who will strengthen and expand existing strong collaborations with the School of Medicine and/or School of Nursing. Environmental Health researchers at Duke benefit from the exceptional wealth of related expertise at Duke (e.g., Superfund Center , Precision Microbiome Engineering Research Center , Environmental Engineering , the Global Health Institute ) and at nearby institutions including universities (UNC Chapel Hill, NC State, and NCCU), governmental agencies (NIEHS, EPA), research organizations (e.g., Research Triangle Institute International), as well as many health, consulting, chemical manufacturing, biotechnology, pharmaceutical, and related industries. The position will be based on Duke University’s main campus in Durham, North Carolina. The successful applicant will contribute to teaching, advising and service activities in the University and the applicant’s professional field. The position includes 9 months of salary support. Teaching will consist of 2.5 courses per year, in the applicant’s area of expertise and in coordination with the Division Chair. Course offerings will be part of the Environment PhD , the Integrated Toxicology and Environmental Health Program , , the professional Master of Environmental Management (MEM), and undergraduate programs . Duke University and The Nicholas School of the Environment are strongly committed to advancing inclusive excellence throughout our research, teaching, and service activities. A diverse faculty – defined broadly as representing a wide range of identities, lived experiences, and perspectives – is a prerequisite for excellence and is essential to driving innovation within our scholarly community. To achieve these goals, it is essential that all members of the community feel valued and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of our community are expected to uphold these values, and we seek to hire faculty who are passionate about increasing the participation and success of individuals from all different backgrounds and communities. Interested candidates should submit the following by 23 October 2026, when the committee will begin review: a one-page cover letter; a curriculum vitae; a three-page research statement that includes a description of past research accomplishments, plans for future research, and their experience and approach to mentoring and supporting  a diverse and inclusive research environment; a one-page statement describing their teaching philosophy, previous experiences, future plans for teaching, and experience and approach to fostering an inclusive learning and mentoring environment for their students; and the names of three references. Contact the search committee chair with any questions: Joel Meyer, joel.meyer@duke.edu Please note that Duke specifically seeks information about past misconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or relevant pending investigations as part of the application process. Disclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will initiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to address them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees during the application review process. Duke is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment opportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy related conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke aspires to create a community built on collaboration, innovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich diversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae a three-page research statement that includes a description of past research accomplishments, plans for future research, and their experienc a one-page statement describing their teaching philosophy, previous experiences, future plans for teaching, and experience and approach to f Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.env.duke.edu Box 90328 Durham NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
