    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32409": {
      "title": "Clarence Korstian Professor of Forest Economics and Management",
      "description": "Duke University, NSOE-Faculty Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, NSOE-Faculty Position ID: Duke-NSOEFaculty-KORSTIAN [#32409] Position Title: Clarence Korstian Professor of Forest Economics and Management Position Type: Tenured & Senior faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Economics / Environmental/Natural Resources Economics Environmental Economics and Policy / Forest Finance , Forest Management Forestry / Forest Finance , Forest Management Appl Deadline: 2026/10/01 23:59:59  (posted 2026/08/04 , listed until 2027/02/04) Position Description: Apply Position Description Clarence F. Korstian Professor of Forest Economics & Management Division of Environmental Social Systems, Nicholas School of the Environment, Duke University Job Location: Nicholas School of the Environment, Duke University, Durham, NC, United States Anticipated Start Date: July 1, 2027 Position Description: The Division of Environmental Social Systems (ESS) at the Nicholas School of the Environment invites applications for the Clarence F. Korstian Professor of Forest Economics & Management. This is a tenured position, awarded to a scholar of excellence in the field of forest resource management, with particular expertise in environmental and resource economics and policy. The successful candidate's background, scholarship, and reputation should reflect experience and training in regional and/or global forest management issues.\r\n\r\nThe position is based at Duke University's main campus in Durham, North Carolina. The successful applicant will have established a distinctive, innovative research program recognized by scholars in the field as exceptional. In addition to exemplary scholarship, the applicant should demonstrate an ability to sustain external funding at a level appropriate to their research, to recruit, train, and matriculate graduate students into relevant and impactful careers, and to provide academic service and leadership within their institution. Teaching Expectations: The standard teaching load for tenured faculty at the Nicholas School is 2.5 courses per year. The successful applicant will teach courses in environmental and resource economics, topics in forest resource management, and advanced empirical or modeling methods aligned with their research agenda and also serve Nicholas School degree programs. One course will be a master's-level introductory course on environmental and resource economics. Remaining courses will be assigned by the ESS Division Chair based on the candidate's topical expertise and the needs of the Master of Forestry (MF) program. The candidate will also provide leadership, oversight, and strategic vision for the MF program, administered by the Nicholas School of the Environment.\r\n\r\nThe successful applicant will also serve as a coursework advisor and Master's Project advisor for students in the Master of Environmental Management (MEM) and MF programs and will participate in doctoral training through the Nicholas School Division of Environmental Social Systems or through the University Program in Environmental Policy (UPEP). Forestry and Environmental Economics at Duke University and the Nicholas School of the Environment: Duke's tradition in forestry dates to 1931, when Clarence F. Korstian became the first director of the Duke Forest and, in 1938, the founding dean of the Duke School of Forestry — the first graduate forestry school in the US South. That legacy continues today through the Nicholas School's Master of Forestry (MF) program, which trains experts in the sustainable management of forested ecosystems and draws on one of the nation's premier university-held research forests. The Duke Forest itself now spans more than 7,000 acres across three North Carolina counties, providing a living laboratory for forest science, economics, and policy research directly adjacent to campus.\r\n\r\nThis position sits at the center of Duke's broader commitment to climate and environmental research, including the Duke Climate Commitment, a university-wide initiative spanning research, education, and operations. The field of environmental and resource economics at Duke is a strength with ten full-time faculty that span the Nicholas School, the Sanford School of Public Policy, and the Department of Economics, offering a rich, interdisciplinary community of scholars. For this position, the Nicholas School values disciplinary contributions to economics, interdisciplinary contributions to forestry and general science, and specialty contributions to forest economics and policy as well as engagement with policy-makers, industry, and the public. \r\n\r\nThe position also connects to the Ph.D. Program in Environmental Policy (UPEP), jointly administered by the Nicholas School and the Sanford School of Public Policy, which trains doctoral students working at the intersection of environmental science, economics, and policy. Qualifications: Basic Qualification: Ph.D. or equivalent degree in forestry, economics, natural resources, public policy, or a related field.\r\n\r\nPreferred Qualifications: Applicants should demonstrate an outstanding record of research and teaching, as well as plans to develop an innovative, distinctive research program that will advance knowledge in forestry economics and management. Applicants should show a record of excellence in teaching and/or mentoring of students, including the ability to mentor students from diverse educational and socioeconomic backgrounds. Application Requirements: A cover letter that highlights the applicant's primary reasons for interest in the position and principal qualifications for it. A current curriculum vitae. A two-page statement of research accomplishments and future research plans, with reference to knowledge frontiers in forest resource management and economics. A two-page statement on teaching and mentoring philosophy, including concrete examples of how the applicant has put their philosophy into practice, and, if available, evidence of teaching effectiveness and examples of teaching or mentoring students from diverse educational and socioeconomic backgrounds. A brief statement (one to two-page) describing experience leading or administering academic programs, centers, or initiatives. Representative publications (up to 3). List of references (to be contacted later in the search process). Applications should be submitted via AcademicJobsOnline, Job #32409.\r\n\r\nQuestions regarding this position may be directed to the Search Committee Chair: Martin Doyle, Nicholas School of the Environment, Duke University; martin.doyle@duke.edu.\r\nTimeline\r\nThe search committee will begin reviewing applications on October 1, 2026. \r\nWho We Are\r\nThe ESS Division is home to tenure-track and non-tenure-track faculty in the social, policy, and management sciences. Division faculty are at the forefront of generating new disciplinary and interdisciplinary knowledge on a range of environmental topics, including climate change, energy, forests, water, pollution, sustainable business, and environmental justice, both in the U.S. and internationally. Faculty fields include, among others, economics, political science, finance, management science, human geography, anthropology, engineering, and hydrology. The Division values faculty engagement with governments, private companies, international organizations, nonprofit civil society organizations, and local communities that promotes more sustainable use of environmental resources.\r\n\r\n\r\nEqual Employment Opportunity\r\nDuke is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment opportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion (including pregnancy and pregnancy-related conditions), sexual orientation, or military status.\r\n\r\nDuke aspires to create a community built on collaboration, innovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on the robust exchange of ideas — an exchange that is best when the rich diversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility to uphold these values.\r\n\r\nFull policy: https://oie.duke.edu/policies-procedures-and-statements/equal-employment-opportunity-statement/ Reference Checks\r\nAs part of Duke's standard hiring process for regular rank faculty, finalists' references will be contacted, and the successful candidate will be asked to complete a reference-check attestation consistent with Duke's faculty hiring policies.\r\n\r\nFull policy: https://facultyaffairs.provost.duke.edu/duke-reference-check-regular-rank-faculty/ Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae 1 representative publication 2 representative publication 3 representative publication two-page statement of research accomplishments and future research plans, with reference to knowledge frontiers in forest resource managemen two-page statement on teaching and mentoring philosophy, including concrete examples of how the applicant has put their philosophy into prac A brief statement (one to two-page) describing experience leading or administering academic programs, centers, or initiatives. Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.env.duke.edu Box 90329 Duke University Durham NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/31954": {
      "title": "Temporary Instructor",
      "description": "Duke University, NSOE-Faculty Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, NSOE-Faculty Position ID: Duke-NSOEFaculty-TINST2627 [#31954] Position Title: Temporary Instructor Position Type: Non-regular rank faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Environmental / Environment Appl Deadline: none  (posted 2026/04/15 ) Position Description: Apply Position Description Generic Job Description for Contract Instructor, Nicholas School of the Environment The Nicholas School of the Environment at Duke University is seeking a qualified instructor for one or more courses to be offered in the 2026/207 Academic year, for students enrolled in the Master of Environmental Management (MEM) program. The two-year MEM is a professional degree, training students for careers focused on solving complex environmental problems. Required Qualifications: PhD degree in a related field; strong teaching experience. The course(s) will be taught in-person at Duke’s Durham, NC campus. To apply: Interested individuals should send the following materials to AcademicJobsOnline - Job #31954 • Cover letter that addresses the candidate’s background, training, and experience in the topics of relevance to MEM students, potentially including:  business and the environment, coastal environmental management, ecotoxicology and environmental health, environmental economics and policy, energy and the environment, ecosystem science and conservation, forestry, natural resource finance, water resources management.  The application package should also include: • details of previous teaching experience; • CV or resume; • Copies of teaching evaluations. Review of applications will begin on May 1, 2026, although applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Duke aspires to create a community built on collaboration, innovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich diversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Duke University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment opportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae evidence of teaching excellence statement of teaching experience with, and commitment to, teaching a diverse student body And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.nicholas.duke.edu 919-613-8081 Box 90329 Duke University Durham NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
