    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32425": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor",
      "description": "Duke University, Pratt Masters Programs Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Pratt Masters Programs Position ID: Duke-GSF-DTIADJ [#32425] Position Title: Adjunct Professor Position Type: Non-regular rank faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Engineering / Design Engineering Appl Deadline: filled (posted 2026/08/07 , listed until 2027/02/07) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Master’s of Engineering (M.Eng) in Design & Technology Innovation (DTI) in the Pratt School of Engineering at Duke University (Durham, NC) invites applications for multiple Non-Regular Rank Adjunct Professor positions. Ideal candidates will have relevant industry experience in one or more of the following, or related, areas: \r\ndesign innovation methodologies, e.g. human-centered design, equity-centered design and design justice, design thinking, speculative design, and/or other related methodologies; \r\nuser experience design, engineering and/or research, e.g. user interface and experience design, front-end engineering, quant/qual user experience research, participatory research methods and co-design, AR/VR experience design, and/or other related areas; \r\ncreative technology and hardware-software development and prototyping, e.g. web/mobile development, no- and low-code prototyping methods, XR development, interactive device design, 2D/3D CAD, digital fabrication, design for manufacturing, and/or other related areas; \r\napplied data science and artificial intelligence for applications in design and innovation. Candidates with graduate degrees, especially at the PhD level, are preferred and strongly encouraged to apply, as are candidates with commensurate industry experience/expertise in related disciplines of engineering, design, data and computer sciences, and related fields. Candidates with teaching/course development experience are strongly encouraged to apply. This position is expected to be part time, but other levels of commitment may be considered. We seek a faculty member who is committed to building a diverse and inclusive community that fosters excellence in teaching. Duke University is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment opportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. Duke also makes good faith efforts to recruit, hire, and promote qualified women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans. Applications must be submitted electronically through https://academicjobsonline.org ; Application Materials Required - Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: \r\nCurriculum Vitae \r\nTeaching Statement \r\nThree References (no actual letters, just names and email addresses)\r\nA link to a digital portfolio (optional, but strongly encouraged) Further Info: https://pratt.duke.edu/ Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://pratt.duke.edu/academics/departments/iene/ 110 Science Drive, Teer 305 Box 90271 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33101": {
      "title": "Associate in Research",
      "description": "Duke University, Psychology and Neuroscience Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Psychology and Neuroscience Position ID: Duke-Psych&Neuro-AIR_OIRHOYLE [#33101] Position Title: Associate in Research Position Type: Other Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708-0999, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Social Psychology Appl Deadline: 2026/12/31 23:59:59  (posted 2026/10/15 , listed until 2026/12/31) Position Description: Apply Position Description Job Summary: Seeking an Associate in Research, jointly supporting the Suffering and Compassion (SC) Lab (50%) and the Office of Institutional Research survey program (50%) at Duke University. Ideal candidates are recent college graduates who are detail-oriented, have excellent communication skills, and have research interests connected to human flourishing, student development, and/or higher education. This position is ideal for individuals looking to gain research experience prior to applying to graduate school, or for those interested in a career in data management and research support. The position will be in-person (Durham, NC, USA), and is a one-year position with a possibility of renewal depending on performance and funding. About the Suffering and Compassion Lab: The SC Lab, directed by Professor Rick Hoyle, is part of the Department of Psychology and Neuroscience at Duke University. The Associate in Research will support the collection, management, and analysis of quantitative and qualitative data for research on beliefs about human suffering and their consequences for well-being and prosocial behavior. About the Office of Institutional Research: The Office of Institutional Research (OIR) advances the university’s mission by transforming data into insight and insight into action. We serve as a catalyst for institutional learning—bringing clarity, rigor, and perspective to the questions that shape our future. In an environment defined by complexity and accountability, IR ensures that strategy is grounded in evidence, decisions are informed by context, and progress is measured with integrity. OIR’s survey program, led by Dr. Molly Weeks, contributes to the university’s mission by providing accurate, timely, and reliable data regarding the lived experience of members of the Duke community, including students, faculty, and staff. The Associate in Research will work closely with the survey program lead to implement survey data collections, prepare data for statistical analysis, integrate self-report data with administrative data sources, conduct data analysis, develop data visualizations, and report results for institutional and external audiences, as appropriate. OIR data collection includes regular surveys of incoming students, graduating seniors, faculty, and staff, as well as ad hoc surveys and analyses supporting institutional priorities. In addition to institutional surveys, the Associate in Research will support the Duke Undergraduate Check-In study [DUCkI], a research initiative examining the diverse processes that contribute to student success. Work Performed: The Associate in Research will perform a variety of tasks related to survey design, planning, and implementation; data management; data analysis; and dissemination of findings internally and, for some projects, in scholarly presentations and publications. Assisting in designing and implementing data collection efforts (15%) - Supporting participant recruitment and retention - Maintaining IRB records Data management (30%) - Developing and maintaining data and process documentation - Managing audio files and transcription quality reviews from interviews - Cleaning and scoring self-report data - Merging self-report data with administrative records data Statistical analysis (descriptive, correlation, and regression-based techniques; qualitative coding and analysis; 30%) Assisting in preparing reports, dashboards, and data visualizations for university leaders and campus partners, with a focus on data integrity, accuracy, and consistency (20%) Assisting in general administrative activities as requested (e.g., scheduling and planning meetings with campus partners and collaborators; 5%) Ensuring work is performed in accordance with relevant data privacy and security policies and ethical standards The above statements describe the general nature and level of work being performed by individuals assigned to this classification. This is not intended to be an exhaustive list of all responsibilities and duties required of personnel so classified. Qualifications Applicants must hold a bachelor’s degree. Preferred Qualifications Excellent organizational skills and keen attention to detail Strong communication and interpersonal skills Ability to work independently and make progress on multiple varied tasks Competency with one or more of the following: Excel, SPSS, SAS, MPlus, R, Qualtrics, Tableau, PowerBI, and/or basic web coding (html, php, css, Python, JavaScript) Research experience and experience with statistical analysis and data visualization Enthusiasm for working collaboratively with administrators, faculty members, and other academic professionals Required application materials: Cover\r\nletter CV/resume Names\r\nand contact information of two references Applicants should upload their\r\nmaterials through Academic Jobs Online. Only\r\napplicants who have applied through Academic Jobs Online will be reviewed. If you are not able to upload your application materials or if letter writers\r\nwould prefer to upload their letters directly, please complete your application\r\nand email those materials to Meghann Koffi at meghann.koffi@duke.edu to be uploaded manually. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Two references (actual letters acceptable but not required, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://psychandneuro.duke.edu/ 417 Chapel Drive Box 90086 Duke University Durham, NC 27708-0086 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32753": {
      "title": "Professor (tenured)",
      "description": "Duke University, Psychology and Neuroscience Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Psychology and Neuroscience Position ID: Duke-Psych&Neuro-PROFESSOR [#32753] Position Title: Professor (tenured) Position Type: Tenured & Senior faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708-0999, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Psychology / Clinical Psychology Appl Deadline: 2026/11/20 23:59:59 * * (posted 2026/09/17 , listed until 2026/11/20) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Department of Psychology and Neuroscience at Duke University, located in Durham, NC, invites applications for a distinguished Full Professor (tenured) position in Clinical Psychology, anticipated to begin Summer 2027. We welcome outstanding scholars of international eminence in the broad domain of clinical psychology and neuroscience to apply for the Marcil-Monahan Family Professorship, a Full Professor Distinguished Chair (endowed) position in the Department of Psychology and Neuroscience at Duke University. The specific research area is open, but preference will be given to applicants employing human neuroscience tools and rigorous quantitative approaches to advance the understanding and improvement of mental health and brain function. The successful applicant will be a world-renowned scholar with a strong record of extramural research funding. We are looking for an effective teacher at the undergraduate and graduate level who has a history of outstanding mentoring of Ph.D. students and other trainees. Eligibility for clinical licensure is desirable, but not essential. The successful candidate is also expected to have a strong record of highly visible professional and academic service, on campus and nationally. Applicants should include a curriculum vitae, statements of research and teaching interests, three selected reprints/preprints, and three contacts for letters of reference. To apply, please complete your application online through the “Apply” link below. Only applicants that have applied through Academic Jobs Online will be reviewed. Applications received by November 15 will be guaranteed consideration. Please direct inquiries to the search chair, Terrie Moffitt (terrie.moffitt@duke.edu). Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Curriculum Vitae Research statement Teaching statement Three Publications - Reprints/Preprints Three references (actual letters acceptable but not required, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://psychandneuro.duke.edu/ 417 Chapel Drive Box 90086 Duke University Durham, NC 27708-0086 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32105": {
      "title": "Husein and LeAnna Cumber Family Professorship",
      "description": "Duke University, Sanford School of Public Policy Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Sanford School of Public Policy Position ID: Duke-Sanford School of Public Policy-CUMBER26 [#32105] Position Title: Husein and LeAnna Cumber Family Professorship Position Type: Tenured & Senior faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Political Science Law History Public Policy Appl Deadline: 2026/08/24 23:59:59  (posted 2026/05/26 , listed until 2026/11/26) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Sanford School of Public Policy at Duke University invites applications for the inaugural Husein and LeAnna Cumber Family Professorship. This search is for an outstanding senior scholar in the field of national and/or international security whose work engages major scholarly debates as well as contemporary policy issues. We intend to appoint a senior scholar at the level of tenured full professor.  The successful candidate will hold a doctorate in a relevant field, such as political science, history, public policy, or law. The Cumber Family Professor will teach courses in their field of expertise and contribute to the Sanford School’s core teaching in its undergraduate and graduate programs in public policy and public affairs.  Candidates who can contribute to the Sanford School's Master of National Security Policy (MNSP), a hybrid mid-career degree program, are particularly encouraged to apply. More information on the MNSP can be found at https://sanford.duke.edu/academics/masters-programs/master-national-security-policy/ The Sanford School includes a full-time faculty of almost 70 and offers an undergraduate major,\r\nthree masters programs, and a Ph.D. program. Current faculty members have degrees in a variety\r\nof disciplines including: demography, economics, history, law, medicine, philosophy, political\r\nscience, psychology/social psychology, public policy, public health/health policy, and sociology. The faculty also includes Professors of the Practice drawn from the policy and related practitioner worlds. The School houses several interdisciplinary research centers and offers degree-related programs and opportunities in Washington, China, Scotland, India, London and Geneva. More information on the Sanford School can be found at https://sanford.duke.edu Candidates should submit a letter of application that addresses their teaching experience, research record and current agenda, and relevant policy experience. Letters should also make clear why they would be interested in joining the Sanford School of Public Policy. The letter, along with a CV and the names of three references (letters from whom will be solicited at the campus-visit stage), should be submitted to Jennifer Siegel, Chair of the Cumber Family Professorship committee, via the following website: https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32105 Applications received by 24 August 2026 will be assured full consideration. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://www.sanford.duke.edu/ Sanford School of Public Policy Box 90239 Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33002": {
      "title": "Lecturing Fellow/Senior Lecturing Fellow",
      "description": "Duke University, School of Law Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, School of Law Position ID: Duke-Law-SPRADJ26 [#33002] Position Title: Lecturing Fellow/Senior Lecturing Fellow Position Type: Non-regular rank faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Law Appl Deadline: 2026/10/31 23:59:59  (posted 2026/10/08 ) Position Description: Apply Position Description About\r\nthe Position: Duke University Law is seeking applicants for multiple adjunct positions.  These positions are short-term, part-time, non-benefits eligible positions.  Selected candidates will teach a single class for\r\nthe Spring Semester of 2026. Candidates must have demonstrated and specialized legal expertise in disability law, legal research and writing, trial practice, or civil advocacy. Responsibilities: Development of course\r\nmaterials that meet the expectations and policies articulated by the Law\r\nSchool’s Curriculum Committee and the Office of Academic Affairs, and as\r\nrequired by the American Bar Association’s accreditation standards.  The\r\ninstructor must be available to teach each week of the 13 weeks during the Spring semester, except as requested or approved by the Office of Academic\r\nAffairs.  The instructor must be available to students outside of teaching\r\nhours, through timely responses to emails and through office hours.  The\r\ninstructor will be responsible for appropriate and timely feedback to students,\r\nincluding evaluation and grading of student coursework. Qualifications: - Graduation\r\nfrom an ABA-accredited law school - Significant practice or policy experience in area of identified\r\nneed - Documented, specialized legal expertise in identified area of\r\nneed - Ability to develop and deliver organized, accurate and coherent\r\nlegal coursework - Effective\r\ncommunication of complex legal information to students both orally and in writing - Effective\r\nand respectful communication with students - Ability to provide responsive and constructive feedback to\r\nstudents Preferences\r\nin hiring include: teaching experience Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: law.duke.edu 919-613-8541 210 Science Drive Suite 1015, Duke Box 90362 Durham, NC 27708-0362 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32415": {
      "title": "Duke University, School of Law",
      "description": "Duke University, School of Law Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, School of Law Program ID: Duke-Law-VAP2728 [#32415] Program Title: Visiting Assistant Professor Program Program Type: Non-regular rank faculty Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Law Appl Deadline: 2026/11/13 23:59:59 * (posted 2026/09/14 , updated 2026/08/05 , listed until 2026/11/13) Description: Apply Description The Visiting Assistant Professor (“VAP”) program supports scholar/teachers interested in appointment as tenure-track members of a law faculty. VAPs are in residence at the Law School for two, or in some cases three, academic years, with the expectation that they will enter the law school teaching market in the fall of their last year as a VAP. A VAP becomes a full member of a robust institutional culture of intellectual engagement and exchange and has many opportunities to develop teaching skills in a program focused on each VAP’s individual interests. Duke Law School has a successful record in placing its VAPs in tenure-track academic positions, as detailed on our website ( https://law.duke.edu/vap/ ). The School expects that while at Duke a VAP will produce at least one work of original scholarship with guidance and mentoring from Duke Law faculty members. Each year, the VAP will teach one upper-level course (or two, if the VAP so prefers) of the VAP’s choice or design, subject to the Law School’s needs. The VAP may teach the same course each year. A VAP is invited to attend all faculty activities open to visiting professors, including faculty workshops and conferences. Each VAP will have at least one opportunity to present a paper in the faculty workshop series. VAPs are also encouraged to propose other opportunities to present their work, including brown-bag sessions with other faculty members, and to engage with colleagues in other schools and departments at Duke University. Each VAP receives an office amidst other members of the faculty as well as administrative and secretarial support. VAPS have no administrative responsibilities. Compensation The annual salary is $70,000, plus benefits (including health insurance), for each year of the position. Each VAP also has a $5,000 faculty account (each year) that can be used for travel to conferences, research, or other academic expenses. The Application Process We invite applications from graduates of any law school and any year \r\nof graduation. We encourage applications from those with experience in \r\nlaw practice. Candidates will be selected based on their potential to \r\nobtain a tenure-track position at a leading law school. If you would like to be considered for a visiting assistant professor \r\nposition beginning in the fall of 2027, please upload your completed\r\napplication materials by November 13, 2026. We will begin reviewing the applications shortly thereafter. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Curriculum Vitae Copies of any scholarly legal articles that the candidate has written and would like to have considered; published, unpublished, or in draft Law school  transcript List of courses candidate is willing to teach (listed in order of  preference) A scholarly agenda outline, with particular emphasis on the scholarship contemplated during the professorship  period Two reference letters (to be submitted online by the reference writers on this site ) Further Info: https://law.duke.edu/vap/ 919-613-7035 210 Science Drive Suite 1015, Duke Box 90362 Durham, NC 27708-0362 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32410": {
      "title": "Doctor of Nursing Practice (DNP) Nurse Anesthesia Faculty",
      "description": "Duke University, School of Nursing Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, School of Nursing Position ID: Duke-School of Nursing-NURSEANESTHESIA [#32410] Position Title: Doctor of Nursing Practice (DNP) Nurse Anesthesia Faculty Position Type: Non tenure-track faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Administrative / Academia Salary Range: N/A Appl Deadline: 2026/10/30 23:59:59 * * (listed until 2026/10/30) Position Description: Apply Position Description Duke University School of Nursing (DUSON) Job Title: Doctor of Nursing Practice (DNP) Nurse Anesthesia Faculty Department: Office of Academic Affairs \r\n  \r\nSupervisor: Vice Dean for Academic Affairs Applications must be received by October 30, 2026. Position Description: The Duke University School of Nursing (DUSON) is a renowned institution committed to advancing nursing education, science, and clinical practice innovations. We are dedicated to health equity, social justice, and preparing nurse leaders, clinical experts, scholars and innovators who strive to promote nurse-led models of care and transformative innovations that enhance health outcomes of patients and teams.  We are currently seeking applications for an experienced doctorally-prepared Certified Registered Nurse Anesthetist (CRNA) to join the faculty of our nationally ranked Doctor of Nursing Practice (DNP) Nurse Anesthesia program.  Preference will be given to those candidates with extensive clinical experience, a record of state or national leadership, and evidence of scholarship. The primary responsibilities of this role include full-time teaching of nurse anesthesia courses, serving as a Chair or committee member for DNP-nurse anesthesia students scholarly projects, and mentoring students in collaboration with the program faculty.  All DNP-nurse anesthesia courses are taught face-to-face on campus. Qualifications: This full-time on-campus position requires three years of experience as a Certified Registered Nurse Anesthetist (CRNA) and a record of success in graduate nursing education.  Preference will be given to those with demonstrated leadership, advocacy efforts and a program of scholarship, which encompasses not only peer-reviewed publications but may also include conference presentations, development of practice guidelines, and implementation of quality improvement projects. Faculty appointment for this position will be made at the Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor level on Track II (a non-tenure earning practice/clinical track) with the expectation that teaching could span across the academic programs at DUSON. The Duke Nursing community includes over 85 full-time faculty members, over 120 staff members and nearly 1,200 students. The school offers four nursing degree programs:1) pre-licensure Master’s of Nursing (MN) program for students with an earned baccalaureate (or higher) degree, 2) Master of Science in Nursing (MSN) and Post-graduate certificate programs, 3) Doctor of Nursing Practice (DNP) that includes the Nurse Anesthesia program, 4) Doctor of Philosophy (PhD program).  Duke University offers an unusually rich, interdisciplinary environment conducive to teaching, practice, and research across the undergraduate, graduate, and professional schools (Nursing, Medicine, Divinity, Business, Law, Engineering, Environment and Policy) and various sponsored institutes within the United States and internationally. Duke is rated by its employees as one of the best places to work in the Triangle area and offers a competitive salary and benefits package. Our Doctor of Nursing Practice programs equip graduates to become trailblazers in clinical practice and healthcare leadership. Consistently recognized among national nursing school rankings, our DNP programs emphasize research, innovation, and transformational leadership to prepare graduates to address complex health challenges and drive meaningful change across the healthcare continuum.  The Duke University School of Nursing DNP program was ranked #4(tie) and DNP CRNA Program was ranked #9 in the most recent U.S. News and World Report rankings of graduate nursing programs. Application Instructions: Applications are due by October 30, 2026. Interested individuals should submit a curriculum vitae and cover letter detailing their specific teaching and clinical experience, areas of scholarship and service, along with the names and contact information of three references to Academic Jobs Online (AJO)  {Link}.  If you have any questions, please contact Dr. Barbara Turner Vice Dean of Faculty Affairs and Development at barbara.turner@duke.edu Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.nursing.duke.edu DUMC 3322 Durham, NC 27710 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33093": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Duke University, Social Science Research Institute Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Social Science Research Institute Position ID: Duke-SSRI-GRIDPOSTDOC [#33093] Position Title: Postdoctoral Associate Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Social Sciences Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/10/15 , listed until 2027/04/15) Position Description: Apply Position Description Occupational Summary The Center on Genomics, Race, Identity, Difference (GRID) in the Social Science Research Institute at Duke University invites applications for a full-time Postdoctoral Associate position. The successful candidate will work directly with Dr. Charmaine Royal (director of GRID) along with a multidisciplinary team to conduct mentored independent and collaborative Ethical, Legal, and Social Implications (ELSI) research. The primary research project is focused on identifying and assessing novel policy implications of ancestry-informed genomic research practices to develop recommendations for precision medicine initiatives. The candidate will also have the opportunity to contribute to other related funded research such as a multi-site project exploring the influences of gene-environment interplay on pain experiences among individuals living with sickle cell disease. This post-doctorate position provides up to 3 years of individualized mentored research and career development, from cultivating a distinctive research agenda to exploring and navigating academic and other relevant career pathways. It is designed to support the development of an early career scientist interested in becoming a leading independent scholar at the intersection of genomics, health equity, and ELSI research. Work Performed \tDesign and conduct normative and/or empirical research on ethical, legal, and policy issues related to ancestry-informed genomic research practices and precision medicine initiatives. \tLead and contribute to manuscripts for publication in top journals and other scholarly outlets relevant to research interests and findings. \tContribute to the development of guidelines, policy recommendations, and educational resources pertaining to research interests. \tPresent research findings and other research outputs to diverse audiences at local, national, and international scholarly and public seminars, conferences, and other meetings. \tLead and participate in the preparation of grant applications. \tOrganize and contribute to research meetings and workshops. \tParticipate in educational activities and mentoring students, as appropriate. \tParticipate in various short-term academic and professional development opportunities, such as courses or workshops that will enhance the scholar’s knowledge and skills relevant to areas of research interest. \tPerform any other related duties incidental to the work described herein. Required Qualifications at this Level Education/Training:\tWork requires communication, analytical, and organizational skills generally acquired through completion of a PhD degree or equivalent doctorate in a field related to the research, such as human genetics and genomics, STS (science, technology, and society) studies, social and behavioral sciences, public health, public policy, bioethics, law, and/or medicine. The doctoral degree must be completed before application submission.\r\n    \r\nExperience: At least six months of applicable post-graduate research experience or any other equivalent combination of relevant education and/or experience. A record of or strong potential for scholarly productivity, including leadership roles on academic publications and presentations. Experience with genomic, qualitative, quantitative, normative, legal, and/or mixed-methods research. Skills:\tStrong written and oral communication skills, including translating interdisciplinary work to various audiences Careful attention to detail Excellent interpersonal, organizational, and project management skills Flexibility and the ability to manage multiple priorities and meet project deadlines Ability to work well both independently and as a member of collaborative and diverse teams Preferred Qualifications\r\n   Training in genomics research, e.g., computational genomics, population genetics, molecular genetics Duke University is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment opportunity without regard to an individual’s age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion (including pregnancy and pregnancy-related conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke aspires to create a community built on collaboration, innovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on the robust exchange of ideas – an exchange that is best when the rich diversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Instructions Interested candidates should submit:\r\n\tCover letter, including a research statement, relevant expertise, and interest in the position\r\n\tCurrent CV\r\n\tA writing sample\r\n\tContact information for two professional or academic references The application deadline is October 31, 2026. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Writing Sample Two references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://ssri.duke.edu/ Duke University Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32805": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Duke University, Social Science Research Institute Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Social Science Research Institute Position ID: Duke-SSRI-HAIPOSTDOC [#32805] Position Title: Postdoctoral Associate Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Social Sciences / Research Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/09/23 , listed until 2027/03/23) Position Description: Apply Position Description Be You. Be Bold. Choose Duke. Postdoctoral Associate Human-AI Research Pipeline (HARP) | Duke University Department/Unit: Social Science Research Institute\tAnticipated Start Date: Nov. 1, 2026\r\nApplication Review Begins: Immediately\t                Location: Durham, North Carolina\r\nWork Arrangement: On-site\t                        Salary: $65,000 Research Program Description The Human-AI Research Pipeline (HARP), part of Duke University's Society-Centered AI Initiative, is an interdisciplinary research program examining how Artificial Intelligence (AI) affects students' cognition, learning, well-being, social experiences, and decision-making. HARP is developing a longitudinal multimodal research resource to study human-AI interaction and its impacts using student-informed, privacy-conscious methods. HARP is led by Dr. Jana Schaich Borg, Associate Research Professor in the Social Science Research Institute. The core development team includes Dr. Rick Hoyle, Professor of Psychology and Neuroscience and Associate Chair of Psychology and Neuroscience; and Dr. Christopher Bail, Professor of Sociology, Computer Science, Political Science, and Public Policy and Director of the Society-Centered AI Initiative.  Other principal investigators across Duke are involved in specific aspects of HARP development, testing, and deployment. Overall HARP Project Goals\r\n●\tExamine when, where, and how students use generative AI and other emerging AI technologies.\r\n●\tAssess relationships between AI use and learning, well-being, cognition, social connection,    decision-making, and educational experiences.\r\n●\tUse participatory design and student co-creation to develop responsible approaches to multimodal data collection, research design, and dissemination.\r\n●\tDesign and deploy a platform for linking measures of AI use to multimodal readouts.\r\n●\tCharacterize how the benefits and harms of AI use vary across contexts and individual differences through core projects and collaborations with other researchers.\r\n●\tProvide a pipeline for researchers across campus to study AI use.\r\n●\tDisseminate findings through scholarly publications, presentations, reports, and engagement with university partners. Position Summary HARP invites applications for a full-time Postdoctoral Associate to help build the HARP research infrastructure and conduct the initial studies leveraging the infrastructure.  The postdoc will design and lead core projects examining the impact of AI interaction on social cognition, emotional regulation, group collaboration, and collaborative learning using eye-tracking, physiology, EEG, and novel computational measures.  The postdoc will also play key roles in developing AI-use measurement techniques and experience sampling measures.  This is an ambitious, fast-moving research program, and the position is well suited to a researcher who is excited to launch and lead multimodal, multi-method studies while simultaneously helping to build new research infrastructure. The postdoc’s primary mentor will be Dr. Jana Schaich Borg, but the postdoc will also work closely with Drs. Rick Hoyle and Christopher Bail, as well as other HARP Principal Investigators in the Pratt School of Engineering involved in the first stage of research studies.  The position will provide opportunities for intellectual mentorship and collaboration with principal investigators across multiple disciplines.  Additionally, the postdoc will work closely with students through the HARP Student Fellows program and through leading focus groups. Primary Responsibilities ●\tLead the experimental design, data collection, and data analysis of initial studies assessing the impacts of human-AI interaction on social cognition, emotional regulation, and collaborative learning using EEG, eye-tracking, physiology, and computational measures.\r\n●\tDevelop and conduct additional research related to human-AI interaction, student AI use, learning, well-being, and social behavior.\r\n●\tContribute to qualitative research, including student focus groups and participatory design activities.\r\n●\tWork with the investigative team to refine research questions, study designs, measures, analytic plans, and publication priorities.\r\n●\tHelp develop, test, document, and maintain HARP research protocols, instruments, and data resources.\r\n●\tApply responsible research practices related to informed consent, participant privacy, sensitive data, data access, and reproducibility.\r\n●\tLead and contribute to manuscripts, presentations, reports, protocols, and grant-related materials.\r\n●\tCoordinate with HARP Student Fellows.\r\n●\tMentor and collaborate with undergraduate and graduate student researchers.\r\n●\tCoordinate effectively with interdisciplinary faculty, staff, students, and institutional partners.\r\n●\tParticipate in professional development and other activities appropriate to a Duke postdoctoral appointment. Required Qualifications ●\tPh.D. or equivalent doctoral degree in neuroscience, psychology, computational social science, or a field relevant to the position, completed before the appointment begins.\r\n●\tDemonstrated experience conceiving of and conducting independent or collaborative research.\r\n●\tExperience conducting experimental, quantitative, computational, or mixed-methods human-subjects research.\r\n●\tExperience collecting and analyzing EEG, physiology, eye-tracking, or related wearable measurements.\r\n●\tStrong statistical analysis skills.\r\n●\tStrong programming skills in a programming language used for data manipulation and statistical analysis.\r\n●\tEvidence of scholarly productivity through publications, presentations, a dissertation, technical reports, software, datasets, or comparable outputs.\r\n●\tStrong written and oral communication skills.\r\n●\tAbility to work independently and collaboratively in a fast-paced interdisciplinary environment. Preferred (but not required) Qualifications ●\tExpertise in social cognition.\r\n●\tExperience studying AI in educational or student contexts.\r\n●\tExperience with longitudinal research methods.\r\n●\tExperience analyzing natural language logs.\r\n●\tExperience with participatory research, co-design, student engagement, or community-engaged research.\r\n●\tExperience in human-centered, responsible, ethical, or society-centered AI.\r\n●\tExperience with IRB submissions, informed-consent materials, data-management plans, or data-use agreements.\r\n●\tExperience mentoring undergraduate or graduate researchers. Appointment Terms ●\tFull-time Postdoctoral Associate appointment.\r\n●\tInitial one-year appointment with the possibility of renewal, contingent on satisfactory performance, project needs, and available funding.\r\n●\tSalary and benefits in accordance with applicable Duke University policies.\r\n●\tPrimary work location: Durham, North Carolina.\r\n●\tAnticipated start date and work arrangement: As soon as a suitable candidate is ready to begin; all work will be completed onsite in Durham, NC. Application Materials ●\tCover letter describing qualifications, research interests, and fit with HARP.\r\n●\tCurrent curriculum vitae.\r\n●\tA 1-2 page research statement about what research related to the job description you would be most excited to pursue, and why you are qualified to lead that research.\r\n●\tA writing sample or comparable research product that you think exemplifies your scholarly work.\r\n●\tContact information for three professional references. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Questions may be directed to Dr. Jana Schaich Borg at js524@duke.edu. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Duke is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment opportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy related conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke aspires to create a community built on collaboration, innovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich diversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Writing Sample Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://ssri.duke.edu/ Duke University Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32474": {
      "title": "IBRC Lab Manager - Associate in Research",
      "description": "Duke University, Social Science Research Institute Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Social Science Research Institute Position ID: Duke-SSRI-IBRCLAB [#32474] Position Title: IBRC Lab Manager - Associate in Research Position Type: Administration Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Social Sciences / Social Sciences Appl Deadline: 2026/09/30 23:59:59 * (posted 2026/08/18 , listed until 2027/02/18) Position Description: Apply *** the listing date or deadline for this position has passed and new applications are no longer accepted. *** Position Description We are hiring a research coordinator to facilitate the activities of grant-funded research (see https://sites.duke.edu/bablab/ ) and the Duke Interdisciplinary Behavioral Research Center (IBRC, https://ibrc.duke.edu/ ), directed by Dr. Patty Van Cappellen at Duke University. The research coordinator will work closely with Dr. Van Cappellen to perform the administrative and research activities of grant-funded projects in her Belief, Affect, and Behavior Lab (BABLab). Current topics are virtual worship and well-being, empathy and religious intergroup relations. The research coordinator will also assist with some of the administrative duties of the IBRC. The IBRC is an interdisciplinary research facility serving faculty and students in social and behavioral sciences. The IBRC provides the infrastructure (laboratory space), equipment (laptop, software, cameras), support (training, research assistant program), participant access (community participants pool), and funding to facilitate social and behavioral research. *it is expected for the candidate to move to NC and be in person, in an office on Duke campus.* Work Performed:\r\n- Assisting in designing and running a series of experiments with human subjects related to multiple grant-funded projects\r\n- Maintaining IRB records\r\n- Cleaning and analyzing data\r\n- Assisting in writing manuscripts\r\n- Maintain lab website and work on public outreach based on the work done in the lab\r\n- Assisting in general administrative activities as requested including planning meetings\r\n- Manage funds for payment of participants.\r\n- Managing and training a team of undergraduate research assistants\r\n- Assisting with media outreach\r\n- Support research activities of the IBRC including maintaining a database of community members and students who wish to participate in paid research at the IBRC. Preferred Qualifications:\r\nIdeal candidate will have a strong undergraduate background in psychology with previous research experience, including SPSS or R and scientific writing. Experience with the following is desirable, but not required: Excel, Qualtrics, CloudResearch or Prolific, Psychophysiology data collection software and equipment such as Mindware or Biopac. Seeking candidate with excellent time-management skills, attention to detail, interpersonal, communication, and writing skills, extremely efficient in email correspondence, ability to work independently, and ability to juggle multiple varied tasks. Experience with web design and marketing is a plus. Candidate should have his or her own transportation for tasks away from the IBRC. It is the expectation that all staff members will demonstrate exceptional workplace behaviors in the execution of their specific position responsibilities. These behaviors are customer focus, collaboration, creative problem solving, continuous learning, and a commitment to diversity. Application Process: Please submit a cover letter describing relevant past research experience and interests (especially regarding stats, coding of participants’ open-ended responses, RA training, involvement in independent research), a writing sample (it can be your honors thesis or a research paper you wrote), a CV, email and phone numbers for two to three professional references, and your relationship to them. Please submit apply and submit all documents by September 2, 2026. Education: Work requires a bachelor's degree in a field related to the specific position. Duke is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment opportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, or veteran status. Duke aspires to create a community built on collaboration, innovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich diversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. To achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the community feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all individuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Essential Physical Job Functions: Certain jobs at Duke University and Duke University Health System may include essential job functions that require specific physical and/or mental abilities. Additional information and provision for requests for reasonable accommodation will be provided by each hiring department. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Writing Sample Two references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://ssri.duke.edu/ 919-681-3936 Duke University Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32256": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Duke University, Social Science Research Institute Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Social Science Research Institute Position ID: Duke-SSRI-RARHEH [#32256] Position Title: Research Associate Position Type: Administration Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Social Sciences / Social Services Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/06/26 , listed until 2026/12/26) Position Description: Apply Position Description DUKE UNIVERSITY, INTERDISCIPLINARY RURAL HEALTH EQUITY HUB Role: Civic Science Fellow, Interdisciplinary Rural Health Equity Hub Context: Interdisciplinary Rural Health Equity Hub at Duke University The Interdisciplinary Rural Health Equity Hub is a Duke University initiative focused on advancing rural health equity through interdisciplinary collaboration, community partnership, and action-oriented scholarship. Bringing together expertise from Nursing, Engineering, Divinity, and the Social Science Research Institute, the Hub works to strengthen connections between research and the priorities, experiences, and expertise of rural communities across North Carolina and beyond. Core Hub activities include a Rural Health Community of Practice, pilot funding provision, and a Regional Rural Incubator that supports community-academic innovation and problem-solving. The Rural Health Equity Hub was selected by the Civic Science Fellowship program to host a Civic Science Fellow for its 2026-2028 cohort. Context: Civic Science Fellows Program The national Civic Science Fellows program ( https://civicsciencefellows.org/ ) brings together an interdisciplinary network of journalists, bench and social scientists, community-facing practitioners, content creators, public-interest organizations, and funders to develop evidence-based, human-centered approaches to build meaningful collaboration between science and diverse communities. The Civic Science Fellows are thought leaders, bridge builders, change agents, and communicators working to learn about community priorities and scientific research, and to bring those worlds together on equal footing to create the conditions for more inclusive and holistic problem-solving that broadens the benefits of science. Fellows are embedded in networked organizations, typically for 18 months, to co-create and pilot cutting-edge civic science projects—forging connections between science, technology, and civic life. Position Summary The Rural Health Equity Civic Science Fellow, based at Duke but part of a national Civic Science Fellow program ( https://civicsciencefellows.org/ ), will help strengthen connections between scientific research and rural communities by supporting collaborative learning, community engagement, partnership development, and knowledge translation activities across the Hub. Working closely with academic and community partners, the Fellow will help identify priorities, facilitate collaboration, and contribute to the co-development of solutions that address rural health challenges. This position is ideal for an individual with applied, academic, or combined experience in research, community engagement, public health, healthcare, rural development, or related fields who is committed to advancing rural health equity through collaborative, community-centered approaches. Specific activities will be informed by the Fellow's expertise and interests, as well as the evolving needs and priorities of community partners and Hub initiatives. Example responsibilities may include: •\tSupporting the Rural Health Community of Practice and related partnership-building activities.\r\n•\tAssisting with planning and facilitation of community-engaged events, workshops, and convenings.\r\n•\tSupporting the Regional Rural Incubator through community engagement, collaborative problem solving, and project development activities.\r\n•\tDeveloping and disseminating practical products such as toolkits, frameworks, implementation guides, briefs, or other translational resources.\r\n•\tContributing to or leading action research, evaluation, or community-engaged projects conducted in partnership with local organizations and communities.\r\n•\tSynthesizing lessons learned and identifying opportunities to strengthen rural health equity efforts across the region.\r\n•\tSupporting grant proposals to strengthen and continue the Rural Health Equity efforts. Qualifications Master's degree or higher strongly preferred. PhD welcome but not required. Candidates should possess either: •\tApplied professional experience relevant to rural health equity, community health, public health, healthcare delivery, community development, or related fields; OR\r\n•\tAcademic experience with demonstrated experience in, or strong potential for, community-partnered or community-engaged research. Candidates should also possess: •\tCommunity engagement and partnership development\r\n•\tFacilitation and collaborative leadership\r\n•\tStrong written and verbal communication skills\r\n•\tAbility to work effectively across diverse stakeholder groups\r\n•\tProject coordination and organizational skills\r\n•\tInterest in rural health, health equity, and community-centered approaches to change\r\n•\tAbility to translate knowledge into practical, actionable resources This role is based in Durham, NC, and may include work in Vance, Granville, and surrounding Counties. A candidate should reside in the local area to be available for in-person activities and needs.\r\nPer the timeline of the Civic Science Fellowship, this Fellowship is planned for 19 months (Sept. 2026-June 2028).  The position is contingent on funding from the Civic Science Fellowship program. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Two references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://ssri.duke.edu/ Duke University Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32932": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Duke University, Statistical Science Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Statistical Science Position ID: Duke-StatSci-ELPDA [#32932] Position Title: Postdoctoral Associate Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Statistics Appl Deadline: 2026/10/08 23:59:59 * filled (posted 2026/10/02 , listed until 2027/07/13) Position Description: Apply *** the listing date or deadline for this position has passed and new applications are no longer accepted. *** Position Description Duke University invites applications for a Postdoctoral Associate position in Statistical Science under the mentorship of Professor Eric Laber. This position will be involved with conducting research at the intersection of reinforcement learning and statistics. The primary responsibility of the candidate will be to advance statistical methodology useful for the design and analyses of micro-randomized trials and experimental designs for Markov decision processes. The candidate is expected to innovate statistical methods and algorithms, create software implementations, carry out numerical experiments, analyze data collected at the lab, and help write manuscripts based on these findings. The candidate is also expected to participate in lab meetings and discussions, be a statistics advisor to other junior lab members, and contribute to the collective intellectual development. A PhD degree in Statistics or a related field is required. To apply, please submit CV, cover letter stating research interests, and the contact information for at least three references to Academic Jobs Online ( https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32932 \"> https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32932 ) as soon as possible. Review will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter with brief statement of teaching experience Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://www.stat.duke.edu/ Box 90251, 214 Old Chemistry Durham, NC  27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32663": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Assistant Research Professor",
      "description": "Duke University, Statistical Science Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Statistical Science Position ID: Duke-StatSci-PARP27 [#32663] Position Title: Postdoctoral Assistant Research Professor Position Type: Non tenure-track faculty Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Statistics Appl Deadline: 2026/10/05 23:59:59  (posted 2026/09/10 , listed until 2027/03/10) Position Description: Apply Position Description The Department of Statistical Science at Duke University, located in Durham, NC, is inviting applications for a Postdoctoral Assistant Research Professor, to begin in Fall 2027.  The ideal applicant has strong skills in teaching introductory statistical science courses for undergraduate students.    Responsibilities include teaching four courses per year.  Applicants must have completed their Ph.D. in Statistics, Biostatistics, Data Science, or a closely related field prior to the date of appointment. Applicants should upload their CV, a brief statement of their teaching experience, and contact information only for at least three references to AcademicJobsOnline.org ( https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32663 \"> https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/32663 ). All applications received by October 5th are guaranteed full consideration, and the position will remain open until filled. The appointment will be for a two year contract  starting July 1, 2027 through June 30, 2029 with the possibility of up to one additional and final year.  Questions should be directed to stat-search@duke.edu. Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Please note that Duke University specifically seeks information about past\r\nmisconduct or policy violations (in the past ten (10) years) and/or\r\nrelevant pending investigations as part of the application process.\r\nDisclosures will not be an automatic bar to hiring but, instead, will\r\ninitiate further dialogue regarding past actions and steps taken to\r\naddress them. Disclosures will not be shared with search committees\r\nduring the application review process. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter with brief statement of teaching experience Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://www.stat.duke.edu/ Box 90251, 214 Old Chemistry Durham, NC  27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
