    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33164": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Duke University, NSOE-ENS & ECS Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, NSOE-ENS & ECS Position ID: Duke-NSOE-ENS & ECS-POSTDOC_NC_JAYASUNDARA [#33164] Position Title: Postdoctoral Associate Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Data Science Molecular Biology Biostatistics Computational Biology Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/10/20 , listed until 2027/04/20) Position Description: Apply Position Description This position will support the completion of a recently funded research project investigating kidney health in study cohorts from Eastern North Carolina. The field-based component of the project has been completed, and biological specimens are now being transferred to Duke University for laboratory processing and analysis. The project requires an individual with specialized expertise in kidney health, molecular analysis of human urine and blood specimens, and the analysis and integration of multi-omics datasets. The successful candidate will be responsible for processing, preparing, and analyzing urine and blood samples collected from study participants. Responsibilities will include the extraction and quality assessment of DNA, RNA, proteins, and metabolites; preparation of samples for downstream molecular and biochemical analyses; and implementation of rigorous quality-control procedures. The position values expertise in kidney health biomarker discovery, validation, and analysis, including established and emerging urinary and blood-based biomarkers of kidney injury and function. Importantly, demonstrated expertise in transcriptomics, proteomics, and metabolomics is sought for this position. The successful candidate should have experience with the statistical analysis of high-dimensional omics datasets, including appropriate approaches for normalization, quality control, differential expression or abundance analysis, multiple-testing correction, pathway and functional enrichment analysis, and biomarker discovery. Expertise in integrating transcriptomic, proteomic, metabolomic, and clinical/phenotypic data using computational and machine-learning approaches is particularly desirable. Relevant experience may include supervised and unsupervised learning, dimensionality reduction, clustering, feature selection, predictive modeling, cross-validation, and multi-omics data integration. The individual will work closely with study investigators and collaborators to connect molecular profiles with measures of kidney health and environmental exposures, with the goal of identifying biomarkers, molecular pathways, and biological signatures associated with kidney injury and disease. Responsibilities will include maintaining reproducible computational workflows, performing statistical and machine-learning analyses, integrating molecular and clinical datasets, interpreting findings, and contributing to scientific reports, presentations, and manuscripts. This position is essential to fulfilling commitments associated with the recently awarded grant. The project has a one-year timeline with the possibility of extension. The primary duties of this position include:\r\nKey Responsibilities 10% - Process, aliquot, and prepare human urine and blood specimens for laboratory and multi-omics analyses. Extract and assess the quality and quantity of DNA, RNA, proteins, and metabolites from biological specimens. Prepare and/or coordinate samples for transcriptomic, proteomic, and metabolomic profiling. 20%  - Measure and analyze established and emerging biomarkers of kidney injury and kidney function. 40% - Perform statistical analysis of transcriptomic, proteomic, metabolomic, and clinical datasets. Conduct differential expression/abundance, pathway, enrichment, and biomarker discovery analyses. Integrate multi-omics, kidney health, environmental exposure, and clinical/phenotypic datasets. Apply machine-learning and multivariate statistical approaches for feature selection, classification, prediction, clustering, and identification of molecular signatures associated with kidney health outcomes. 10% - Develop and maintain rigorous, reproducible computational and bioinformatic workflows. Implement quality assurance and quality-control procedures for both biological specimens and resulting molecular datasets. Maintain accurate specimen inventories, laboratory records, analytical documentation, and research databases. 20% - Interpret molecular and computational findings in collaboration with project investigators and multidisciplinary collaborators. Contribute to scientific reports, presentations, manuscripts, and other grant deliverables. Term: 1 year, fulltime Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Curriculum Vitae Two manuscripts representing your skills Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://nicholas.duke.edu/ Grainger Hall 9 Circuit Drive, Box 90328 Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33165": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Duke University, NSOE-ENS & ECS Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, NSOE-ENS & ECS Position ID: Duke-NSOE-ENS & ECS-POSTDOC_SRILANKA_JAYASUNDARA [#33165] Position Title: Postdoctoral Associate Position Type: Postdoctoral Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Areas: Molecular Biology Data Science Biostatistics Computational Biology Appl Deadline: (posted 2026/10/20 , listed until 2027/04/20) Position Description: Apply Position Description This position will support the completion of a recently funded research project investigating kidney health in study cohorts from Sri Lanka. The field-based component of the project has been completed, and biological specimens are now being transferred to Duke University for laboratory processing and analysis. The project requires an individual with specialized expertise in kidney health, molecular analysis of human urine and blood specimens, and the analysis and integration of multi-omics datasets. The successful candidate will be responsible for processing, preparing, and analyzing urine and blood samples collected from study participants. Responsibilities will include the extraction and quality assessment of DNA, RNA, proteins, and metabolites; preparation of samples for downstream molecular and biochemical analyses; and implementation of rigorous quality-control procedures. The position values expertise in kidney health biomarker discovery, validation, and analysis, including established and emerging urinary and blood-based biomarkers of kidney injury and function. Importantly, demonstrated expertise in transcriptomics, proteomics, and metabolomics is sought for this position. The successful candidate should have experience with the statistical analysis of high-dimensional omics datasets, including appropriate approaches for normalization, quality control, differential expression or abundance analysis, multiple-testing correction, pathway and functional enrichment analysis, and biomarker discovery. Expertise in integrating transcriptomic, proteomic, metabolomic, and clinical/phenotypic data using computational and machine-learning approaches is particularly desirable. Relevant experience may include supervised and unsupervised learning, dimensionality reduction, clustering, feature selection, predictive modeling, cross-validation, and multi-omics data integration. The individual will work closely with study investigators and collaborators to connect molecular profiles with measures of kidney health and environmental exposures, with the goal of identifying biomarkers, molecular pathways, and biological signatures associated with kidney injury and disease. Responsibilities will include maintaining reproducible computational workflows, performing statistical and machine-learning analyses, integrating molecular and clinical datasets, interpreting findings, and contributing to scientific reports, presentations, and manuscripts. This position is essential to fulfilling commitments associated with the recently awarded grant. The project has a one-year timeline with the possibility of extension. The primary duties of this position include:\r\nKey Responsibilities 10% - Process, aliquot, and prepare human urine and blood specimens for laboratory and multi-omics analyses. Extract and assess the quality and quantity of DNA, RNA, proteins, and metabolites from biological specimens. Prepare and/or coordinate samples for transcriptomic, proteomic, and metabolomic profiling. 20%  - Measure and analyze established and emerging biomarkers of kidney injury and kidney function. 40% - Perform statistical analysis of transcriptomic, proteomic, metabolomic, and clinical datasets. Conduct differential expression/abundance, pathway, enrichment, and biomarker discovery analyses. Integrate multi-omics, kidney health, environmental exposure, and clinical/phenotypic datasets. Apply machine-learning and multivariate statistical approaches for feature selection, classification, prediction, clustering, and identification of molecular signatures associated with kidney health outcomes. 10% - Develop and maintain rigorous, reproducible computational and bioinformatic workflows. Implement quality assurance and quality-control procedures for both biological specimens and resulting molecular datasets. Maintain accurate specimen inventories, laboratory records, analytical documentation, and research databases. 20% - Interpret molecular and computational findings in collaboration with project investigators and multidisciplinary collaborators. Contribute to scientific reports, presentations, manuscripts, and other grant deliverables. Term: 1 year, fulltime Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Curriculum Vitae Two manuscripts representing your skills Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: https://nicholas.duke.edu/ Grainger Hall 9 Circuit Drive, Box 90328 Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
