    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33208": {
      "title": "Associate in Research",
      "description": "Duke University, Physics Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Physics Position ID: Duke-Physics-AIRSCOLNIC [#33208, 51230771] Position Title: Associate in Research Position Type: Other Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Physics Appl Deadline: 2026/10/28 23:59:59 * (posted 2026/10/22 ) Position Description: Apply Position Description Position Description: The Duke Cosmology Group in the Department of Physics at Duke University invites applications for an Associate in Research position. The successful candidate will work with Prof. Dan Scolnic. \r\nThe group seeks a creative and motivated researcher to work on frontier space policies, with a focus on planetary defense on international and local scale.\r\nThe researcher will analyze how governments, international bodies, and local communities prepare for and respond to potential asteroid and comet impacts. The work will start with existing frameworks, such as the International Asteroid Warning Network (IAWN) and the Space Mission Planning Advisory Group (SMPAG), as well as national and local emergency management practices. The position offers the opportunity to draft policy briefs and develop early-stage governance proposals in a highly interdisciplinary environment which connects law, public policy, risk communication, and planetary science. Ideal candidates have strong analytical and problem-solving skills, clear writing, and curiosity about emerging space policy and law.\r\nIdeal candidates should have strong problem-solving skills, curiosity about emerging space policies and laws. Responsibilities:\r\n● Analyze international and national frameworks for planetary defense governance\r\n● Assess local emergency-management plans for potential asteroid and comet impacts\r\n● Lead and mentor undergraduate and graduate students on policy research projects\r\n● Collaborate with teams across the Cosmology Research Group\r\n● Contribute to early-stage policy proposals in an interdisciplinary environment\r\n● Present results at collaboration meetings, policy forums, and scientific conferences Required Qualifications:\r\n● Bachelor’s degree in public policy, international relations, law, political science, physics, astronomy, or a related field by the start date. Master’s degree preferred.\r\n● Strong background in science and technology policy, space law, or risk governance Environment and Collaboration:\r\nThe Duke Cosmology Group is part of a vibrant research community with strengths and presence in leading Stage-IV Dark Energy experiments. We are actively involved in the LSST Dark Energy Science Collaboration, NASA OpenUniverse effort and in several Roman Project Infrastructure Teams (PITs), offering ample opportunities for collaboration and leadership.\r\nDuke University is in Durham, North Carolina, a thriving area with an excellent quality of life and strong academic networks across the Research Triangle. Application Instructions:\r\nApplicants should submit the following materials as a single PDF to [insert application email or link]:\r\n1.\tCover letter outlining research interests and qualifications 2.\tCurriculum vitae including publications 3.\tBrief research statement (≤3 pages) 4.\tLink(s) to relevant open-source software contributions (e.g., GitHub) 5.\tContact information for three references Review of applications will begin October 22, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled.\r\nInformal inquiries are welcome and may be sent to Dr. Dan Scolnic (daniel.scolnic@duke.edu). Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.phy.duke.edu Duke University Physics Department Box 90305 Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
    },
    "https://academicjobsonline.org/ajo/jobs/33206": {
      "title": "Associate in Research",
      "description": "Duke University, Physics Skip to main content AcademicJobsOnline .org ‌ View Jobs Events Registered Employers Contact Us Login Duke University, Physics Position ID: Duke-Physics-AIRVOSSEN [#33206, 51230770] Position Title: Associate in Research Position Type: Other Position Location: Durham, North Carolina 27708, United States of America [ map ] Subject Area: Physics Appl Deadline: 2026/10/28 23:59:59 * (posted 2026/10/22 ) Position Description: Apply Position Description Position Description: The Medium Energy Physics Group in the Department of Physics at Duke University invites applications for an Associate in Research position. The successful candidates will work with Prof. Vossen\r\nThe Associate in Research will work on finalizing the Belle II analysis on hadron correlation in jets. The goal is to submit a publication on this topic. Required Qualifications: ● Bachelor’s degree in physics or a related field by the start date. Master’s or Ph.D. degree preferred. Environment and Collaboration: Duke University is in Durham, North Carolina, a thriving area with an excellent quality of life and strong academic networks across the Research Triangle. Application Instructions: Applicants should submit the following materials as a single PDF to AJO:\r\n1.\tCover letter outlining research interests and qualifications 2.\tCurriculum vitae including publications 3.\tBrief research statement (≤3 pages) 4.\tContact information for three references Review of applications will begin November 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Informal inquiries are welcome and may be sent to Dr. Vossen (anselm.vossen@duke.edu). Duke University\r\n is an Equal Opportunity Employer committed to providing employment \r\nopportunity without regard to an individual's age, color, disability, \r\ngender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, \r\nnational origin, ethnicity, race, religion, sex (including pregnancy and pregnancy \r\nrelated conditions), sexual orientation, or military status. Duke University aspires to create a community built on collaboration, \r\ninnovation, creativity, and belonging. Our collective success depends on\r\n the robust exchange of ideas—an exchange that is best when the rich \r\ndiversity of our perspectives, backgrounds, and experiences flourishes. \r\nTo achieve this exchange, it is essential that all members of the \r\ncommunity feel secure and welcome, that the contributions of all \r\nindividuals are respected, and that all voices are heard. All members of\r\n our community have a responsibility to uphold these values. Application Materials Required: Submit the following items online at this website to complete your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae Research statement Three references (no actual letters, just names and email addresses ) And anything else requested in the position description. Further Info: www.phy.duke.edu Duke University Physics Department Box 90305 Durham, NC 27708 © 2026 AcademicJobsOnline.Org. All Rights Reserved."
